window.COURSE={"title":"Detecting and Stopping Bot Attacks in eCommerce","lessons":[{"title":"Introduction to eCommerce Bot Attacks"},{"title":"Types of Bot Attacks"},{"title":"Signs and Symptoms of a Bot Attack"},{"title":"Traditional Bot Management Techniques"},{"title":"Current Bot Management Solutions"},{"title":"Strategic Considerations for Bot Management"},{"title":"Partnering with Solution Providers"},{"title":"Review"}],"slides":[{"kind":"image","lessonIndex":-1,"title":"Course Overview","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"ddc0b319-4d7c-441c-a48a-d91935b596f3\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #000000; text-decoration: inherit\">Detecting and Stopping Bot Attacks in eCommerce explores bots' complex and dynamic ecosystem&nbsp;and their vast, often hidden, impact on eCommerce. These automated threats are behind massive customer data breaches and hundreds of millions of dollars lost to fraud each year. They harm the customer experience and cause long-term damage to brands. They lower companies’ revenues while raising their operating costs. They are now threatening key IP by scraping proprietary AI models and APIs. Given these stakes, an effective bot defense is of interest far beyond Security and IT teams. It is a critical foundation to safeguard the future of a business.</span></span></p><p></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #000000; text-decoration: inherit\">Participants will learn about different types of attack and their impacts, understand the motivations of the attackers, and signs and symptoms their business may have a bot problem. They will become knowledgeable about how AI is fueling these threats, what defenses are effective today, and key features to look for in a bot mitigation provider.</span></span></p><p></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">Estimated completion time: 2-2.5 hours</span></span></p><p></p></div>","source":["course.description"],"id":"BOTQA-1","originalScreens":[{"lesson":0,"screen":1}],"layout":"conceptVisual","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-1.svg","alt":"Seven lessons and a review: introduction to bot attacks, types of bot attacks, signs and symptoms, traditional techniques, current solutions, strategic considerations, partnering with providers."}},{"kind":"intro","title":"Introduction to eCommerce Bot Attacks","objectiveTitle":"Learning Objectives","items":[{"title":"By the end of this lesson, learners will be able to:","date":"","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"2ddb1cbc-3ea9-4445-a799-a0a40ed23bf2\"><ul><li><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Define bots and explain their history</span></p></li><li><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Explain the significance of bots on eCommerce platforms</span></p></li></ul></div>"}],"image":{"src":"assets/LearnUpon_Thumbnail_Templa-1.jpg","alt":""},"caption":"<p><strong>Lesson 1 of 8</strong></p>","source":["cl2u8rtxg00s11u9tdcjb68oj","cl5cxjsq5000u356q29pqz5a3","cl2u8rtxg00s01u9t4kqz6yoq"],"lessonIndex":0,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-2","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":1}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"61ee0bcb-e99c-4b31-beed-347f7d2c7758\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">In the fast-evolving landscape of eCommerce, bot attacks remain a persistent and unresolved threat, despite having existed for decades. AI is now fueling even more sophisticated attacks, allowing automated software programs to exploit vulnerabilities and overwhelm systems. These attacks can cause significant damage and revenue loss for eCommerce businesses, while also harming customers in numerous ways and eroding brand loyalty.</span></p></div>\n<div data-editor-id=\"3234d3df-4ca1-4c34-b3d1-25a0e5b580f2\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #00AEEF; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>Bot attacks</strong></span> involve automated scripts or programs (bots) that perform tasks on the internet at high speed and volume. These tasks can range from benign activities like web scraping and automated form submissions to more malicious actions such as credential stuffing, carding, and denial of service (DoS) attacks. Specifically targeting online payment systems, these malicious bot attacks can lead to unauthorized transactions and data breaches, posing serious risks to the security and integrity of eCommerce platforms.</span></p></div>","source":["clzllqd7m003s0gfuanj62c5e","clnt5ybxp00fm356ytu90cz9n","clly032r3007d3570cx388u2q"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"Bot Attacks","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-3","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":2},{"lesson":1,"screen":3}],"image":{"src":"assets/Untitled design - 2024-08-.png","alt":""},"caption":""},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bot attacks on eCommerce platforms have a multifaceted impact, affecting financial stability, customer trust, operational efficiency, and competitive positioning. Understanding the scope and consequences of these attacks is essential for businesses aiming to protect their assets and maintain a secure online environment.&nbsp;</span></p><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Let&rsquo;s look at the history of bot attacks and how they have impacted eCommerce platforms.&nbsp;</span></p>","source":["cly3f8nyy00f2357573szmzbx"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"Bot Attacks","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-4","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":4}],"layout":"lessonVisual","image":{"src":"assets/lesson-1-screen-3.png","alt":"A storefront connected to symbols for financial loss, operational disruption, security risk, and customer trust."}},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzllsf0q00jy3575319aqep6","clzls711e01yh35756n1d82en","clu1kj7tq01g4356xo3ptef81"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"History of Bot Attacks","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-5","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":5},{"lesson":1,"screen":6},{"lesson":1,"screen":7}],"leadHtml":"<p>Bot attacks have changed as online services and defensive tools have evolved.</p>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":"","display":"sequence","done":"Bots began as simple helpers and grew into a commercial toolkit for abuse. Today, judge automation by its permission, behavior, and business impact.","items":[["The emergence of bots","Simple programs, such as chat room bots, automated repetitive tasks."],["Automation and early abuse","Automation spread to both useful administration and abuse such as spam."],["DDoS attacks and the botnet era","Compromised devices were coordinated to send spam or disrupt services."],["More sophisticated automation","Tools began replaying credentials and imitating real browsers."],["The current landscape","Tools, proxies, stolen credentials, and solver services are for sale, and AI helps some attackers."]]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"ddc4f942-a625-46c6-b06f-58f58bafa32e\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Bot attacks are not isolated incidents; behind every bot is an individual with a specific objective. Gaining insight into the adversary's motivations and tactics is crucial to developing a robust defense. These individuals can be anyone, but many are often young adults in high school or college. Since not all botting activities are illegal, it's common for consumers to leverage bots to secure in-demand items like limited-edition sneakers or concert tickets.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">It's equally important to distinguish between illegal bot activities, such as account cracking, and actions that, while undesirable to a business, may not be illegal, like automating \"add to cart\" functions to claim inventory. Understanding these nuances helps in crafting a more effective security strategy.</span></p></div>","source":["cm2nnfkh700i53579zkgzggbp","clzllw6up012w3575ivo154ce"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"The Minds Behind Bots","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-6","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":8}],"image":{"src":"assets/source/your-paragraph-text-72-.png","alt":"Open hand beneath a network of people"}},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm2nnj26g00m83579zg1p3n38","clzllxokx004e0ec4fpb18h1a"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"Adversary Motivations","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-7","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":9},{"lesson":1,"screen":10}],"leadHtml":"<p>Attackers automate activity for different reasons.</p>","display":"match","done":"Attackers re-tool and try again while attacks stay easy, cheap, and scalable. Make your business slow and costly to attack, and they move on to easier targets.","pairs":[["Earning a living","Taking over accounts to resell goods and steal loyalty points","Financial motives include account theft, resale, data collection, and loyalty or promotion abuse."],["Bragging rights","Breaching a famous brand's defenses to gain status in hacker forums","Some botters want the challenge and prestige of beating well-known brands."],["Political and social","Posting from fake profiles to sway public opinion at scale","Bots generate posts that look like real users to spread a view or disinformation."],["Access to attack services","Renting packaged tools instead of building an attack from scratch","Cybercrime-as-a-Service packages tools and services, which lowers the barrier to attack."]]},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>A fraud team sees bot attacks now coming from many low-skill actors rather than a few experts. What best explains this shift?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>Browsers now include automation that attackers can switch on with no setup.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Leaked account data now arrives with scripts that run attacks on their own.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"2","title":"<p>Ready-made bot tools and services are sold cheaply, so little skill is needed.</p>","correct":true},{"id":"3","title":"<p>Payment providers share testing tools that attackers reuse for large abuse.</p>","correct":false}],"feedback":"<p>Cybercrime-as-a-Service sells bot tools, proxies, and CAPTCHA-solving services cheaply, so attackers need little technical skill. Browser automation still needs scripting, and leaked data or provider test tools do not run attacks on their own.</p>","multi":false,"source":["cm2yz7ide03zd0h9p5fl97dy8","cm2yz7ide03zc0h9p04dkgh8j"],"lessonIndex":0,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-8","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":11}],"fixedOrder":true},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bot attacks pose a significant threat to eCommerce platforms, disrupting operations, compromising security, and damaging customer trust. These automated programs, designed to execute various malicious tasks, can severely impact an online business&#39;s financial health, operational efficiency, and reputation. </span></p><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">Where the damage shows</p><ul><li>Financial: lost sales, fraud losses, chargebacks, and higher infrastructure costs.</li><li>Operational: slow pages, outages, and teams pulled into incident response.</li><li>Reputation: frustrated customers, distorted reviews, and lower trust in the brand.</li></ul></div>","image":{"src":"assets/stock-image.jpg","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["clzllwu35014e3575y5tdedoy","cm2nnf7sb00hm3579n9ed7bcq"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"Impact on eCommerce Platforms","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-9","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":12}]},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm2noz7pt01se3579mbum4399","cm2nni6vh00iz3579c3bi4bdf"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"Financial Impact","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-10","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":13},{"lesson":1,"screen":14}],"leadHtml":"<p>Bot abuse can affect revenue and costs in different ways.</p>","display":"sort","cats":["Money or assets lost","Higher operating costs"],"items":[["Fraudulent transactions, account takeovers, and chargebacks",0,"These drain revenue directly and add investigation and resolution costs."],["Stolen loyalty points converted into gift cards",0,"Attackers take value from customers and the business, and trust suffers."],["Scraped pricing, content, and proprietary data",0,"Lost intellectual property weakens your competitive edge."],["Ongoing spend on bot management tools and audits",1,"Defending against sophisticated bots requires continuous investment."],["Higher cloud hosting bills from bot traffic",1,"Infrastructure must scale to carry traffic that brings no revenue."],["Per-request fees on payment or reservation platforms",1,"Bots inflate request volumes that third parties charge for."],["SMS verification charges for fake sign-ups",1,"Every fake account can trigger a paid verification message."]]},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzlm11ta016q3575thrwghl5","clzlm0k1y015l3575knspb33h"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"Operational Impact","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-11","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":15}],"leadHtml":"<p>Bot activity can place pressure on systems and teams.</p>","display":"sort","cats":["Downtime and performance","Inventory disruption"],"items":[["The site crashes during a traffic flood and sales stop",0,"Overwhelmed sites become unresponsive, and frustrated customers move to competitors."],["Pages load slowly for genuine shoppers",0,"High bot traffic degrades performance and the customer experience."],["Popular items sit in carts that never check out",1,"Bots hold stock without buying, which keeps it from real customers."],["Stock levels no longer match real demand",1,"False stock levels complicate fulfillment and lead to lost sales."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Loss of Customer Trust","date":"","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"46a4559a-c468-4a98-9d67-33c4ade4a915\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Frequent bot attacks can erode customer confidence in the platform's security and reliability. Customers who experience fraudulent transactions or who frequently find the website down may lose trust in the eCommerce platform and take their business elsewhere. </span></p><p> &nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Maintaining customer trust is crucial for long-term success, and bot attacks can significantly damage this trust.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --9.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Negative Publicity","date":"","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"10196dae-8691-4c2a-b4cb-335152562151\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">High-profile breaches and attacks attract negative media attention, further harming the brand's reputation. Publicized security incidents can lead to a loss of consumer confidence and harm the company’s image. </span></p><p> &nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Effective communication and swift action are essential to mitigate the reputational damage caused by bot attacks.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3.png","alt":""}}],"source":["clzlm3846019e3575fi1vvgut","clzlm2j56017h3575vow64z99"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"Reputation Impact","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-12","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":16}],"display":"accordion"},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzlscit802ca35753zl5jyxb","clzlp0wtl01me3575upwhhg9q","cllwuljci004o35706uo2o209"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"Importance of Understanding and Mitigating Bot Attacks","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-13","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":17},{"lesson":1,"screen":18},{"lesson":1,"screen":19}],"leadHtml":"<p>Understanding the threat helps a merchant choose a proportionate response. Combine prevention, detection, response, and recovery, because no single technology eliminates automated abuse.</p>","display":"match","pairs":[["Proactive defense","Map sensitive journeys and close bypass paths before broad enforcement","Testing controls before they go live prevents gaps and surprises."],["Enhanced fraud detection","Connect account, request, and payment evidence and check it against outcomes","Scores and unusual behavior are clues, not proof."],["Consumer protection","Protect customer data with access controls, encryption, and minimization","Encryption reduces exposure, but it does not make every breach harmless."],["Regulatory compliance","Work with legal and privacy teams to document data use and controls","Using a security tool alone does not establish compliance."],["Business continuity","Rehearse escalation, recovery, and rollback across teams","Practiced procedures protect availability and legitimate transactions."]]},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>During a flash sale, page load times triple and checkout errors rise, but completed orders fall. What does this most likely show?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>Bots are using server capacity, so genuine shoppers cannot finish orders.</p>","correct":true},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Genuine demand is above forecast, which always lowers completed orders.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"2","title":"<p>Search engine crawlers are indexing new product pages faster than usual.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"3","title":"<p>Card issuers are declining payments, which slows pages for all visitors.</p>","correct":false}],"feedback":"<p>Rising load and errors with falling orders points to automated traffic consuming capacity. Genuine demand would usually raise completed orders, well-behaved crawlers are rate-controlled, and issuer declines do not slow page loads for every visitor.</p>","multi":false,"source":["clzr9nf7100ph0g41bgby6z5c","clzr9nf7100pg0g41hf0hftva"],"lessonIndex":0,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-14","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":20}],"fixedOrder":true},{"id":"BOT-SCENARIO-1","lessonIndex":0,"kind":"scenario","character":"assets/scenario-characters/lesson-1.png","title":"Scenario: The Launch That Stalled","name":"Daniel","role":"eCommerce operations lead","stages":[{"context":"You are Daniel, the operations lead for an online retailer. During a product launch, visits surge, checkout slows, and completed orders fall. Marketing calls the traffic a success.","prompt":"What do you do first?","options":[{"text":"Treat the traffic increase as success and keep the launch running without further investigation.","good":false,"outcome":"Checkout delays continue, and genuine customers abandon purchases. Visitor totals hid an operational problem; traffic alone did not show whether the launch was succeeding."},{"text":"Compare traffic patterns, checkout performance, and completed orders before deciding how to respond to the surge.","good":true,"outcome":"You identify repeated automated requests alongside genuine shoppers. Linking technical signals to lost sales gives the team evidence for a targeted response."},{"text":"Block all visitors from unfamiliar locations and keep those restrictions throughout the entire product launch.","good":false,"outcome":"Some genuine shoppers lose access while automated requests continue from other locations. A broad geographic rule creates customer friction without resolving the underlying pattern."}]},{"context":"The team confirms repeated automated requests are consuming checkout capacity. Customer support reports abandoned purchases.","goodLead":"Your evidence gives the response team a clear starting point.","badLead":"The first response cost valuable time, but the team now has clearer evidence.","prompt":"How do you direct the response?","options":[{"text":"Restore checkout capacity, target abusive requests, and track completed purchases alongside customer complaints during recovery.","good":true,"outcome":"Checkout stabilizes as abusive traffic is constrained. Monitoring sales and customer complaints helps confirm that the response protects the business as well as the infrastructure."},{"text":"Keep reporting total visits, wait for traffic to decline, and postpone changes until after the launch.","good":false,"outcome":"The team loses more orders while waiting. A falling request count later would not recover missed sales or demonstrate that genuine customers could complete checkout."},{"text":"Take every storefront offline, clear the traffic dashboards, and restart the launch without checking customer impact.","good":false,"outcome":"The shutdown stops genuine sales too, and clearing dashboards removes useful evidence. Recovery needs a targeted response and measures of customer and business impact."}]}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"f206e03e-086f-4629-a99b-81d67cc3cdfc\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Bot attacks are a longstanding yet unresolved issue in eCommerce, growing more sophisticated as attackers automate more of the customer journey. These threats can cause financial damage, disrupt operations, and erode customer trust.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Modern malicious automation ranges from simple scripted requests to sophisticated activity that imitates legitimate shopping. AI-assisted tools can help attackers develop or adjust scripts and react to defenses, but not every advanced bot uses AI. Evaluate the specific journey at risk, such as login, account creation, product discovery, inventory reservation, checkout, loyalty redemption, or payment, and compare suspicious activity with business context before labeling it an attack.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-15.svg","alt":"Takeaways: bot attacks are long-standing and growing, they cause financial, operational, and reputational harm, AI helps some attackers, evaluate the journey at risk."},"caption":"","source":["cl5h7jsk0000x356q41ft0aq1","clzlp01vb01lz3575zesrepbl","cl2l0bjqk00873a6uebia40e9"],"lessonIndex":0,"title":"Summary","superHeadings":[],"nextHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 20px;\">In the next lesson, we will explore types of bot attacks.&nbsp;</span></p>","id":"BOTQA-15","originalScreens":[{"lesson":1,"screen":21}],"layout":"conceptVisual"},{"kind":"intro","title":"Types of Bot Attacks","objectiveTitle":"Learning Objectives","items":[{"title":"By the end of this lesson, learners will be able to:","date":"","description":"<ul><li><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Explain types of good bots</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Identify types of malicious bots and their attacks</span></li></ul>"}],"image":{"src":"assets/LearnUpon_Thumbnail_Templa-2.jpg","alt":""},"caption":"<p><strong>Lesson 2 of 8</strong></p>","source":["cl2u8ou9808d21v875dtpfu0f","cl5h2leqs009c3571py51oz47","cl2u8ou9708d11v87clou9n33"],"lessonIndex":1,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-16","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":1}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"342921dd-3635-4353-9ef2-e856dcbd3917\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #00AEEF; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>Bots</strong></span>, short for \"robots,\" are automated software programs created to execute specific tasks over the internet with minimal or no human intervention. These tasks can vary widely in complexity, from simple, repetitive actions like web scraping or automating form submissions to more sophisticated processes that closely mimic human behavior, such as interacting with websites, making decisions based on data, or even communicating with users.</span></p></div>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The versatility of bots allows them to be used for both benign purposes, like enhancing productivity and efficiency, and malicious activities, such as launching cyberattacks or committing fraud.</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (34).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["clzra7gwl02g43574kfuc0a8i","cllvb5aab001735703gog7hko","cloxipn3h01ir356x82fmb9ke"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Types of Bot Attacks","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-17","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":2},{"lesson":2,"screen":3}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<p>Beneficial automation supports search, monitoring, and authorized user tasks. Harmful automation abuses access, harms customers, or disrupts the business. The operator name or a human-looking browser is not enough to establish trust.</p><p>Distinguish search indexing, AI training collection, and user-directed shopping agents. A merchant may allow one purpose and restrict another. Verify identity where supported, then apply purpose-specific permissions and limits. A verified operator is not blanket authorization for every action.</p>","source":["clvzqf9le05d33579tvum1eax","clvzqf54p05cp3579u41dlfc2"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Good Bots Vs. Bad Bots","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-18","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":4}],"layout":"comparison","image":{"src":"assets/good-vs-bad-bots.png","alt":"A blue bot with a check mark represents good bots; a red bot with a cross represents bad bots."}},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"process","items":[{"title":"Search and Monitoring","description":"<p>Search crawlers index permitted content; monitoring services check availability. Verify the claimed operator using supported validation methods rather than a user-agent name alone.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --10.png","alt":""}},{"title":"AI Crawlers","description":"<p>AI services may collect content for training or retrieve information for answers. Define which purposes and paths are permitted. Robots dot txt communicates crawler preferences; it is not an authentication or access-control mechanism.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --13.png","alt":""}},{"title":"User-Directed Agents","description":"<p>An agent may browse or transact for a customer. Confirm the permitted action, account permissions, and applicable transaction controls. Recognition of an agent provider does not establish that every end user or request is trustworthy.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --11.png","alt":""}}],"source":["clzra9zr202hg35749jmnz433","clyrsg2v301320g9qcb0c1g64"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Good Bots","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-19","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":5},{"lesson":2,"screen":6}],"display":"topics","leadHtml":"<div data-editor-id=\"0a3ebb2f-cdc9-474b-8ab3-db1ddb2c2fcc\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #00AEEF; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>Good bots</strong></span> are designed to perform beneficial and legitimate functions. They assist users, improve the efficiency of internet services, and provide valuable data to businesses. Some common examples of good bots include:&nbsp;</span></p></div>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":""},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"d1d3e6ad-362a-4228-ae25-bc92f2ab5e76\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 174, 239); text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>Bad bots</strong></span>, in contrast to their benign counterparts, are designed with malicious or unethical intentions. Their purpose is often to commit fraud. These bots are deployed by cybercriminals, fraudsters, or competitors to exploit vulnerabilities, damage business operations, and compromise the security and privacy of user data.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">The impact of bad bots on online services can be severe, leading to financial losses, disrupted user experiences, and a tarnished reputation for affected businesses. Understanding the different types of bad bots is crucial for developing effective strategies to protect against their harmful activities.</span></p></div>","source":["clzreqwzt01mz35754wx24lh8","clzreqwzt01mz35754wx24lh8"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Bad Bots","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-20","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":7}],"image":{"src":"assets/source/untitled-design-2024-07-7.png","alt":"Malware warning symbols on a screen"}},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>Your logs show a crawler calling itself Googlebot requesting product pages at a steady pace. What should you do?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>Block it, since crawlers that request product pages are usually scrapers.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Allow it without checks, since only real search engines can use that name.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"2","title":"<p>Add a CAPTCHA to product pages so that the crawler has to prove it is human.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"3","title":"<p>Verify it is genuine, for example with a reverse DNS check, before trusting it.</p>","correct":true}],"feedback":"<p>Search engine bots crawl and index content, which most merchants want. But bad bots often fake well-known crawler names, so verify the source, for example with reverse DNS, before allowing it. Blocking real crawlers hurts search visibility, and CAPTCHAs would stop genuine crawlers too.</p>","multi":false,"source":["clzu13x5300es0e7fck1iflpy","clzu13x5300er0e7fbkkpg3qj"],"lessonIndex":1,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-21","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":8}],"fixedOrder":true},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Bots operate with high speed, consistency, and scalability, making them highly efficient for various legitimate purposes, such as web indexing, automated customer service, and personal assistance. However, when used maliciously, bots can become powerful tools for executing various harmful activities against online platforms. &nbsp;</span></span></p>","source":["clzrf3y5o02ne0h5w1k5510zw","clyxg3nnd02770e8wffi12zkb"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Types of Bad Bots","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-22","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":9}],"layout":"lessonVisual","image":{"src":"assets/lesson-2-screen-7.png","alt":"Malicious bots targeting storefront accounts, shopping carts, and payments."}},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"4b22ef47-573a-4d7b-9062-5981074af888\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #00AEEF; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>Scraper bots</strong></span> are specialized automated programs designed to systematically extract data from websites, often without the website owner's knowledge or consent. These bots can gather a wide range of information, including product prices, descriptions, inventory levels, customer reviews, etc.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">The data collected by scraper bots is typically used for competitive intelligence, market analysis, and other commercial advantages. While some scraping activities may be legitimate, such as those performed by search engines, unauthorized scraping can have serious negative consequences for businesses.&nbsp;</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (35).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["clzrgcazn004n0g9ggqjif4s9","clzrgcazn004m0g9geeygexos"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Scrapers","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-23","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":10}]},{"kind":"image","variant":"tabs","source":["clzrgquuq02v83575j3mahrc3","clzrgcazn004o0g9ggvqv73zc"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Attack Mechanism","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-24","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":11}],"contextTitle":"Scrapers","html":"<p><strong>Data Extraction</strong></p><p>Scrapers collect content through pages or APIs, sometimes imitating browsing. Public availability does not settle contractual, privacy, or intellectual-property questions. Define permitted collection and protect sensitive endpoints with enforceable controls. Robots dot txt expresses crawling preferences but does not prevent access.</p>","layout":"conceptVisual","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-24.svg","alt":"Four steps: bot requests pages or APIs, imitates normal browsing, extracts content and prices at scale, data is reused by competitors or resellers."}},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzrgcazn004s0g9gfayqdtw7","clzrgcazn004r0g9gflk64zlj"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Impacts","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-25","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":12}],"contextTitle":"Scrapers","leadHtml":"<p>Scraper bots harm a business in several ways.</p>","display":"match","done":"Scraping costs you content, competitive position, site performance, and money. Treat it as a business risk, not just a technical nuisance.","pairs":[["Intellectual property theft","Your product descriptions and images appear on another site without permission","Reused content dilutes the brand and can lead to legal disputes."],["Competitive disadvantage","A rival undercuts your prices within minutes of every change","Real-time visibility into pricing and inventory lets competitors react first."],["Increased server load","Pages slow down for customers during heavy scraping runs","High request volumes degrade the experience and can crash the site."],["Increased operational costs","Per-request fees from a third-party platform climb with no new sales","Scraping inflates third-party fees and infrastructure spend."]]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"59557fd8-8b8b-4252-a7de-e78629c842a0\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #00AEEF; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>Spammer bots</strong></span> are automated programs designed to distribute unsolicited content across various online platforms, including fake reviews, comments, or promotional messages. Their primary objective is to manipulate public perception, deceive consumers, and promote specific products or services.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">These bots can significantly distort the online environment, leading to a range of negative consequences for both businesses and consumers.&nbsp;</span></p></div><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">Warning signs</p><ul><li>A sudden burst of reviews or comments with similar wording or timing.</li><li>Many new accounts posting links or promotions soon after sign-up.</li><li>Ratings that shift sharply without a matching change in sales or returns.</li></ul></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (36).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["clzrgsl4i009k0h8h7lsy54jr","clzrgsl4i009j0h8he8k7fku5"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Spammers","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-26","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":13}]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"tabs","items":[{"title":"Fake Review and Comments","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Spammer bots often create multiple fake accounts to flood online platforms with deceptive reviews and comments. For example, they may post a high volume of positive reviews for their own products or services, artificially inflating their ratings and making them appear more favorable than they actually are.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Conversely, they can also target competitors by posting negative reviews, attempting to damage their reputation and sway potential customers away. This manipulation of online reviews creates a distorted view of product quality, misleading consumers and skewing their purchasing decisions.&nbsp;</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-2.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Promotional Spamming","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In addition to fake reviews, spammer bots are used to flood forums, social media, blogs, and other online platforms with promotional content or spam links. These bots can post irrelevant or repetitive messages that promote specific products, services, or websites, often in an attempt to drive traffic to certain pages.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">This not only degrades the user experience by cluttering online spaces with unwanted content but can also have broader implications. For instance, search engines may penalize websites associated with excessive spamming by lowering their rankings, which can reduce the site&#39;s visibility and traffic.&nbsp;</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-5.png","alt":""}}],"source":["clzrgsl4i009m0h8hg2ej93s8","clzrgsl4i009l0h8hc7lnfk9s"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Attack Mechanism","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-27","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":14}],"display":"topics","contextTitle":"Spammers"},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzrgsl4i009p0h8h2o9l8li9","clzrgsl4i009o0h8hdspt9m2w"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Impacts","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-28","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":15}],"contextTitle":"Spammers","leadHtml":"<p>Spammer bots cause harm that reaches customers, the brand, and search visibility.</p>","display":"match","done":"Spam misleads buyers, damages trust in the brand, and can push the site down in search results.","pairs":[["Misleading consumers","A shopper buys a poor product because of hundreds of fake five-star reviews","Fake reviews paint an inaccurate picture of quality and lead to poor decisions."],["Damage to brand reputation","A sudden wave of negative comments targets a competitor's product","Negative spam campaigns erode trust and sales, even when the claims are false."],["SEO penalties","Search engines push the site down after spammy links appear on it","Search engines penalize sites associated with spam, which cuts organic traffic."]]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"7574ffad-f521-4e04-8aff-3be8d7b945df\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #00AEEF; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>Credential stuffing bots</strong></span> are malicious tools used to gain unauthorized access to user accounts by exploiting the widespread habit of password reuse across multiple websites. These bots leverage stolen username and password combinations, often obtained from data breaches, to infiltrate accounts on various platforms.</span></p></div><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">Warning signs</p><ul><li>A spike in failed logins spread across many accounts and addresses.</li><li>Successful logins followed by quick changes to email, password, or address.</li><li>Customer reports of orders or reward redemptions they did not make.</li></ul></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (37).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["clzrh9n9q00di0h8hekbd9wvr","clzrh9n9q00dh0h8h6x8sguds"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Credential Stuffers","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-29","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":16}]},{"kind":"image","variant":"tabs","source":["clzrh9n9q00dk0h8hftq4agim","clzrh9n9q00dj0h8hgibdewwt"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Attack Mechanism","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-30","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":17}],"contextTitle":"Credential Stuffers","html":"<p><strong>Automated Login Attempts</strong></p><p>Credential stuffing replays previously exposed username and password pairs; credential guessing tries possible values. Attackers can spread attempts over addresses and time, so a quiet IP is not proof of safety. Combine account-level and network patterns with authentication controls. Protect recovery and reset flows as well as login.</p>","layout":"conceptVisual","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-30.svg","alt":"Four steps: attacker obtains leaked username and password pairs, bots replay them across many accounts, attempts are spread over addresses and time, successful logins lead to account takeover."}},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzrh9n9q00dn0h8h3bue8957","clzrh9n9q00dm0h8h8psvgh7g"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Impacts","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-31","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":18}],"contextTitle":"Credential Stuffers","leadHtml":"<p>Credential stuffing harm builds in a chain, from one account to the whole business.</p>","display":"sequence","done":"Each successful login can lead to fraud, then to refunds, legal costs, lost customers, and reputational damage.","items":[["Bot logs in with leaked credentials","A reused password lets the bot into a genuine customer's account."],["Account is taken over","The attacker steals data, changes details, or reaches linked accounts."],["Fraudulent transactions follow","Purchases, transfers, or redemptions are made from the account."],["Business absorbs the loss","Refunds, fraud handling, legal fees, and lost customer trust follow."]]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"59e40fdc-7f02-438a-8880-d529bd19269a\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #00AEEF; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>Scalper bots</strong></span> have become a notorious tool in eCommerce, particularly for high-demand items such as event tickets, limited edition merchandise, and exclusive product releases.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">These bots are designed to automate the purchasing process, enabling their operators to acquire large quantities of sought-after goods within seconds of their release, often outpacing genuine customers. The result is an artificial scarcity of products, which scalpers then exploit by reselling these items at significantly higher prices, often on secondary markets.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (41).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["clzrfog3y01ws3575595su3au","clzrfnm2v01vn3575orjr8xlg"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Scalpers","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-32","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":19}]},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzrf3y5o02ng0h5w7xoqdza0","clzrffhcr01ux35756e1qgq2q"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Attack Mechanism","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-33","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":20},{"lesson":2,"screen":21}],"contextTitle":"Scalpers","leadHtml":"<p>Scalper bots complete the entire purchase process in milliseconds, far faster than any human buyer, and use several techniques to avoid limits.</p>","display":"match","pairs":[["Multiple IP addresses","Limits on how many purchases come from a single source","Rotating addresses spreads purchases and evades IP-based controls."],["Multiple user accounts","Purchase limits set for each individual account","Many accounts, each buying independently, multiply the volume acquired."],["CAPTCHA bypass","Human verification checks during checkout","Solver services and machine learning help bots pass these challenges."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Artificial Scarcity","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Scalper bots quickly deplete the available inventory of high-demand items, creating a false sense of scarcity. This manipulation drives up demand, allowing scalpers to resell the products at inflated prices on secondary markets, which distorts the market and harms genuine consumers.</span></p>","image":null},{"title":"Consumer Frustration","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 17px;\">The rapid purchase of items by scalper bots leads to widespread consumer frustration. Legitimate buyers are often unable to purchase desired products at the original price, and many are forced to buy from resellers at higher prices, leading to feelings of unfairness and dissatisfaction.</span></p>","image":null},{"title":"Damage to Brand Reputation","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Frequent scalper bot activity can tarnish a brand&#39;s reputation. Consumers may perceive the brand as either complicit in or unable to combat the issue, resulting in negative publicity, a loss of customer trust, and potential declines in customer engagement and sales.</span></p>","image":null}],"source":["clzrf3y5o02nj0h5w03mt707y","clzrf819901ts3575m7ypjeyx"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Impacts","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-34","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":22}],"display":"accordion","contextTitle":"Scalpers"},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"23810d40-4906-4a67-8358-79db48f5a112\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #00AEEF; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service) bots</strong> </span>represent one of the most disruptive and damaging tools in the arsenal of cybercriminals. These bots are specifically programmed to execute DDoS attacks, which aim to cripple websites or online services by overwhelming them with an excessive volume of traffic.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">This form of attack can have devastating consequences for businesses, from immediate operational disruptions to long-lasting financial and reputational damage.</span></p></div><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">In practice</p><ul><li>Work with your hosting, CDN, or network provider on DDoS protection before you need it.</li><li>Plan for application-layer attacks that look like normal page or API requests.</li><li>Agree an incident plan that covers communication with customers and teams.</li></ul></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (38).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["clzrg43pq003o0h8hgiewgrss","clzrg43pq003n0h8hda2pa6tp"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) Bots","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-35","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":23}]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"tabs","items":[{"title":"Overview","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">At the core of a DDoS attack is the concept of traffic flooding. DDoS bots, often organized into a botnet&mdash;a network of compromised computers controlled by a single attacker&mdash;are directed to send an enormous number of requests to a target server. These requests can be simple pings, HTTP requests, or more complex queries designed to consume as much of the target&#39;s computational resources as possible.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The traffic generated by a botnet can be so massive that it exhausts the target&#39;s bandwidth and server resources within minutes, leading to two primary effects:</span></p>","image":null,"isOverview":true},{"title":"Bandwidth Exhaustion","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The sheer volume of incoming traffic can saturate the network bandwidth available to the target, making it impossible for legitimate users to reach the website or service. This is particularly effective in targeting smaller websites with limited bandwidth resources.&nbsp;</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-7.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Server Resource Exhaustion","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Even if the target has ample bandwidth, the server&#39;s processing power and memory can still be overwhelmed. Servers are forced to allocate resources to handle each incoming request, and when the volume becomes too high, the server can slow down dramatically or crash entirely, leading to downtime.&nbsp;</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-4.png","alt":""}}],"source":["clzrg43pq003q0h8hfya243cm","clzrg43pq003p0h8hczjhen9e"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Attack Mechanism","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-36","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":24},{"lesson":2,"screen":25}],"display":"topics","contextTitle":"Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) Bots"},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzrg43pq003t0h8h6iom3lc9","clzrg43pq003s0h8ha2zl3ldj"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Impacts","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-37","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":26}],"contextTitle":"Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) Bots","leadHtml":"<p>A DDoS attack affects day-to-day operations, finances, and reputation.</p>","display":"sort","cats":["Operational disruption","Financial losses","Reputational damage"],"items":[["Customers cannot complete checkout while the site is offline",0,"An unresponsive site stops transactions immediately."],["Staff stop planned work to restore service",0,"Response work reduces productivity across teams."],["Emergency spend on DDoS protection and outside experts",1,"Mitigation costs add to the lost sales."],["Revenue lost during prolonged downtime",1,"Businesses that rely on online sales lose income for every hour offline."],["Customers start to doubt the brand is reliable",2,"Repeated outages erode confidence and trust."],["Loyal customers move to a competitor",2,"Lost trust leads to churn that lasts beyond the attack."]]},{"kind":"image","html":"<p>Card-testing bots try to establish whether stolen or enumerated card details can be used. They may target card setup, account verification, or payments; small purchases are one method rather than the whole attack.</p><p>Repeated declines and occasional approvals can create fees, disputes, and poor payment outcomes. Review attempts across sessions and accounts, and coordinate the response with payment and fraud teams.</p><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">Warning signs</p><ul><li>Many small authorizations or card setups with high decline rates.</li><li>The same account, device, or session trying many different cards.</li><li>Rising issuer declines, fraud alerts, or fees from your payment provider.</li></ul></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (39).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["clzri68fp00ke0g6g539p4syt","clzri68fp00kd0g6g1obc8uoh"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Carding Bots","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-38","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":27}]},{"kind":"image","variant":"tabs","source":["clzri68fp00kg0g6g81ns2m80","clzri68fp00kf0g6g6u6x5fbk"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Attack Mechanism","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-39","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":28}],"contextTitle":"Carding Bots","html":"<p><strong>Card Setup and Payment Testing</strong></p><p>Automated testing can use card setup or payment attempts to learn which details work. Examine repeated attempts and authorization outcomes across accounts, devices, sessions, and networks. Limit abusive attempts server-side and use payment-provider controls. A successful challenge or small transaction amount does not establish that a payment is legitimate.</p>","layout":"conceptVisual","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-39.svg","alt":"Four steps: bots obtain stolen or generated card details, test them through card setup or payment attempts, record which details are approved, use or sell the working cards."}},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzri68fp00kj0g6gg7xo4opg","clzri68fp00ki0g6ggpe8dli6"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Impacts","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-40","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":29}],"contextTitle":"Carding Bots","leadHtml":"<p>Card testing creates costs well beyond the fraudulent order.</p>","display":"match","done":"Card testing leads to fraud losses, chargebacks and processor penalties, and a security review. Stopping it early protects cardholders and your card acceptance.","pairs":[["Financial fraud","A validated card is used for a large purchase before it is flagged","Testing confirms which cards work so they can be used for bigger fraud."],["Chargebacks","Funds are returned and the processor raises your fees","Merchants lose the sale and may face penalties or loss of card acceptance."],["Data security concerns","The team reviews gaps in transaction monitoring and bot defense","Successful testing shows where controls need strengthening."]]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"6b6307ac-159e-40db-b3dc-41608cc4e0ae\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #00AEEF; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>Inventory hoarding bots</strong></span> are malicious tools used to manipulate online inventory levels by placing items in shopping carts without completing the purchase. These bots aim to create false scarcity, disrupt competitors, and frustrate legitimate customers by making products appear out of stock.</span></p></div><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">Warning signs</p><ul><li>High-demand items show as out of stock while few orders are completed.</li><li>Many carts hold the same products and are refreshed just before they expire.</li><li>Cart activity comes from new or linked accounts with no purchase history.</li></ul></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (40).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["clzri6fqj00jl0h8h3cej10le","clzri6fqj00jk0h8h4o3a5tdi"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Inventory Hoarding Bots","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-41","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":30}]},{"kind":"image","variant":"tabs","source":["clzri6fqj00jn0h8h0pjy5bw3","clzri6fqj00jm0h8hcxhtb8zk"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Attack Mechanism","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-42","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":31}],"contextTitle":"Inventory Hoarding Bots","html":"<p><strong>Cart Hoarding</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Inventory hoarding bots operate by adding items to online shopping carts and then holding them there without completing the checkout process. By keeping these items in the cart, the bots effectively reserve the inventory, preventing other customers from purchasing the prducts.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In many cases, these bots are programmed to periodically refresh the cart or re-add the items if the cart expires, ensuring that the products remain unavailable for genuine buyers. This tactic creates an artificial scarcity that can disrupt a business&rsquo;s inventory management and sales processes.</span></p>","layout":"conceptVisual","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-42.svg","alt":"Four steps: bots add high-demand items to carts, hold the items without paying, re-add items when carts expire, genuine shoppers see items as out of stock."}},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Artificial Stockouts","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">One of the most immediate effects of inventory hoarding bots is the creation of artificial stockouts. Legitimate customers who try to purchase popular items may find them unavailable due to the perceived shortage created by the bots.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">This false scarcity can drive customers to competitors or secondary markets where the products may be available, albeit at higher prices. The loss of sales opportunities due to these artificial stockouts can be particularly damaging during peak shopping periods or when launching new products.</span></p>","image":null},{"title":"Revenue Loss","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Inventory hoarding bots directly impact a business&rsquo;s revenue by tying up stock that could otherwise be sold to genuine customers. When items are hoarded in shopping carts and not purchased, they remain unavailable for actual transactions, leading to lost sales.&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Over time, this can result in significant revenue loss, especially if the affected products are in high demand.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Additionally, businesses may incur costs related to restocking and managing inventory that appears to be out of stock but is actually being hoarded by bots.</span></p>","image":null},{"title":"Customer Trust Issues","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Repeated instances of stockouts, particularly for popular or limited-edition items, can lead to customer frustration and a decline in trust. Customers expect businesses to manage their inventory effectively, and when they consistently encounter unavailable products, they may start to doubt the reliability of the retailer.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">This erosion of trust can have long-term consequences, as dissatisfied customers are less likely to return and may share their negative experiences with others, further damaging the brand&rsquo;s reputation.</span></p>","image":null}],"source":["clzri6fqj00jq0h8h7l8v9kj7","clzri6fqj00jp0h8h1r2a6r9d"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Impacts","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-43","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":32}],"display":"accordion","contextTitle":"Inventory Hoarding Bots"},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>A competitor matches your price changes within minutes, and API traffic from rotating addresses has tripled. What is most at risk?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>Your customer accounts, which bots are taking over with leaked passwords.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Your pricing and product content, which scrapers are copying at scale.</p>","correct":true},{"id":"2","title":"<p>Your inventory, which bots are holding in carts to create false scarcity.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"3","title":"<p>Your website uptime, which bots are trying to overwhelm with traffic.</p>","correct":false}],"feedback":"<p>Fast price matching and heavy API requests from rotating addresses point to scraping, which copies pricing, content, and other intellectual property. The other attacks would show different signs, such as failed logins, held carts, or outages.</p>","multi":false,"source":["clyxg3nnd02780e8welen8ylr","clzreus3j01o43575cya5p8r1"],"lessonIndex":1,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-44","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":33}],"fixedOrder":true},{"id":"BOT-SCENARIO-2","lessonIndex":1,"kind":"scenario","character":"assets/scenario-characters/lesson-2.png","title":"Scenario: A Sale Without Sales","name":"Amara","role":"fraud operations analyst","stages":[{"context":"You are Amara, a fraud analyst during a limited-stock sale. Products appear unavailable, yet paid orders stay low. Logs show sessions repeatedly reserving items and refreshing carts.","prompt":"Which activity do you investigate first?","options":[{"text":"Investigate a distributed denial-of-service attack because unavailable products always mean the entire storefront is overwhelmed.","good":false,"outcome":"The storefront remains responsive. Focusing only on availability delays investigation of the repeated reservations that are keeping stock away from paying customers."},{"text":"Investigate scalping through completed bulk purchases because every unavailable product must already have been sold.","good":false,"outcome":"Paid-order records do not show bulk purchases. The inventory is being held rather than bought, so a completed-purchase investigation misses the active abuse."},{"text":"Investigate inventory hoarding because repeated cart reservations can make available products appear sold out to shoppers.","good":true,"outcome":"You trace the false scarcity to carts that repeatedly hold stock without payment. The evidence points to inventory hoarding rather than completed scalper purchases."}]},{"context":"You confirm automated cart refreshes are keeping reservations alive. Genuine customers cannot access the affected stock.","goodLead":"Your classification points the team to the reservation flow.","badLead":"After reviewing the reservation records, you identify the affected flow.","prompt":"What response fits this attack?","options":[{"text":"Disable search-engine indexing and leave cart reservations unchanged so fewer automated visitors can find the products.","good":false,"outcome":"Search visibility falls, but the abusive reservations remain active. The response targets a beneficial discovery flow rather than the inventory-hoarding mechanism."},{"text":"Constrain repeated abusive reservations, release expired holds, and monitor genuine checkout completion throughout the sale.","good":true,"outcome":"Stock returns to genuine buyers as abusive holds are constrained. Tracking completed purchases helps the team distinguish successful recovery from another misleading inventory change."},{"text":"Raise the product price for all shoppers and leave reservation rules unchanged throughout the limited-stock sale.","good":false,"outcome":"Genuine shoppers face higher prices while automated sessions keep holding stock. A price change does not address repeated reservations without completed payment."}]}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bots are automated programs that can serve either beneficial or harmful purposes online. Good bots help with tasks such as web crawling and monitoring, while bad bots support abuse such as scraping, spamming, credential stuffing, account takeover, loyalty abuse, scalping, DDoS, and payment-card testing.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Modern automated abuse can target browser, mobile-app, and API flows, including login, registration, product, inventory, checkout, loyalty, and payment endpoints. Different attacks may share automation techniques but create different business risks, so classification should reflect the affected customer journey and business outcome, not just the presence of automation.</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-45.svg","alt":"Takeaways: bots can be good or bad, bad bots target many journeys, attacks span browser, app, and API traffic, classify by journey and outcome."},"caption":"","source":["cl2i5dzh300323a6jyjea5sgx","cloowo8g30197356yo09wxj46","cl2l075h0000c3a6u925yihmk"],"lessonIndex":1,"title":"Summary","superHeadings":[],"nextHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 20px;\">In the next lesson, we will dive into the signs and symptoms of a bot attack.</span></p>","id":"BOTQA-45","originalScreens":[{"lesson":2,"screen":34}],"layout":"conceptVisual"},{"kind":"intro","title":"Signs and Symptoms of a Bot Attack","objectiveTitle":"Learning Objectives","items":[{"title":"By the end of this lesson, learners will be able to:","date":"","description":"<ul><li><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Identify the signs of a bot attack</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Describe the KPIs used to monitor bot attacks</span></li></ul>"}],"image":{"src":"assets/LearnUpon_Thumbnail_Templa-3.jpg","alt":""},"caption":"<p><strong>Lesson 3 of 8</strong></p>","source":["cl2u8qixm08d91v878zp57l82","cl2i6egg0005t3a6jxceoil07","cl2u8qixm08d81v871ii73noo"],"lessonIndex":2,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-46","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":1}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bot attacks are increasingly sophisticated and can manifest in various ways. Detecting these attacks early requires understanding the signs and symptoms that bots typically exhibit. Recognizing these symptoms promptly can be crucial for mitigating damage and protecting your digital infrastructure.&nbsp;</span></p>","source":["clw0sjwgb0an33579dix6jk9n","cl2997e19000z3a6jyy1frr9v"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Signs and Symptoms of a Bot Attack","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-47","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":2}],"layout":"lessonVisual","image":{"src":"assets/lesson-3-screen-2.png","alt":"A monitoring dashboard showing a traffic spike, increasing errors, and suspicious accounts."}},{"kind":"activity","source":["clz01hpfm00sf0hc6f6o95rlr","clzu1rsna049235758j908swj"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Increased Traffic and Load","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-48","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":3},{"lesson":3,"screen":4}],"leadHtml":"<p>One of the most common signs of a bot attack is a sudden, unexplained surge in traffic or server load.</p>","display":"imghot","image":{"src":"assets/lesson-3-screen-2.png","alt":"Monitoring dashboard showing a traffic spike, rising request bars, and an alert"},"pins":[{"x":77.5,"y":28,"title":"Traffic anomalies","text":"Significant, unexplained spikes in visitors or page requests, especially when they do not match marketing activity, new content, or seasonal trends, can indicate bot activity."},{"x":86,"y":18,"title":"Server overload","text":"Increased CPU or memory use, slower response times, or server crashes often point to an overload caused by bot traffic."},{"x":45,"y":66,"title":"Bandwidth saturation","text":"Rapid consumption of bandwidth can suggest large-scale data extraction, such as scraping, or an attempt to overwhelm the server."}]},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzu2dtwf00lu0haa5vpa884e","clzu2dtwe00lt0haahpnk132v"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Unusual User Behavior Patterns","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-49","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":5},{"lesson":3,"screen":6}],"leadHtml":"<p>Bots often display patterns of behavior that deviate from those of genuine users. Compare signals across accounts and sessions before acting.</p>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":"","display":"match","pairs":[["High frequency of requests","Repeated requests spread across addresses to stay under each limit","Distributed attacks can remain below per-address thresholds, so compare across accounts and longer windows."],["Odd navigation patterns","Jumping quickly between unrelated products and the same pages again and again","Bots often follow paths that genuine shoppers rarely take."],["Low engagement metrics","Very short sessions that mobile users and assistive tools can also produce","Correlate low engagement with account and transaction outcomes before acting."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"cards","items":[{"title":"Account Lockouts","front":"<p><strong>Account Lockouts</strong></p>","description":"<p>Lockouts can reflect attacks, forgotten passwords, or faulty rules. Preserve a recovery path so attackers cannot easily exclude genuine customers.</p>"},{"title":"Location Changes","front":"<p><strong>Login Attempts from Unknown Locations</strong></p>","description":"<p>Travel, VPNs, and mobile networks change apparent location. Correlate location with account history and other evidence before restricting access.</p>"},{"title":"Login Outcomes","front":"<p><strong>Unsuccessful Login Rates</strong></p>","description":"<p>Compare login outcomes, recovery, and confirmed compromise. Distributed attacks may make one attempt per account across many accounts.</p>"}],"source":["clzu2htpa04v33575t2plvotg","clzu2xmnt04yd3575tv8avsxc","clzu2gfe300ss0ha0fz4856c9"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Increase in Failed Login Attempts","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-50","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":7},{"lesson":3,"screen":8},{"lesson":3,"screen":9}],"display":"topics","leadHtml":"<p>Investigate login patterns across accounts and networks. Failures can signal abuse, but legitimate errors and rule problems can produce similar symptoms.</p>","leadImage":{"src":"assets/Untitled design - 2024-08--1.png","alt":""},"leadCaption":""},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzu2llk300v50e7f7aim07ja","clzu2llk300v40e7f843b07ul"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Fake Account Creation","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-51","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":10},{"lesson":3,"screen":11}],"leadHtml":"<p>Bots may create fake accounts to spam, scrape data, or commit fraud. Spotting them early prevents bigger problems.</p>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":"","display":"match","pairs":[["Spike in new account creation","Hundreds of quiet accounts created weeks before a holiday sale","Bots often create and \"age\" accounts ahead of big sales so they look legitimate later."],["Suspicious email domains","Many sign-ups using disposable or obscure email providers","Disposable and obscure domains across many new accounts are a red flag."],["Incomplete or incoherent information","Profiles with random names and most details left blank","Minimal or nonsensical profile information often indicates a bot-created account."]]},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzu2sed700wu0e7fdkslfrld","clzu2sed700wt0e7f7v77cffw"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Unusual Checkout and Purchase Behavior","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-52","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":12},{"lesson":3,"screen":13}],"leadHtml":"<p>Bot attacks on checkout often involve scalping or inventory hoarding.</p>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":"","display":"match","pairs":[["Rapid purchase attempts","Dozens of orders for a limited sneaker release within seconds","Quick, repeated purchases of high-demand items suggest scalper bots."],["Cart abandonment","A spike in abandoned carts holding limited-edition items","Hoarding bots hold stock in carts without completing the purchase."],["Discrepancies in inventory","Stock levels that no longer match recorded sales","Unexplained gaps between inventory and sales can indicate manipulation."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"tabs","items":[{"title":"Frequent Error Messages","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">An increased frequency of HTTP 500 errors or other server-side errors can indicate resource exhaustion due to bot traffic.&nbsp;</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-28.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Downtime or Slow Performance","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decreased website performance, longer page load times, or complete unavailability of services can occur when servers are overwhelmed by bot traffic.&nbsp;</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-23.png","alt":""}},{"title":"System Crashes","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Unexplained crashes or server reboots can be triggered by excessive resource consumption from bot activities.&nbsp;</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-24.png","alt":""}}],"source":["clzu34pmw01080ha02t6n8mez","clzu34pmw01070ha0buxvchib"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Unexpected Errors and Downtime","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-53","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":14},{"lesson":3,"screen":15}],"display":"topics","leadHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bot attacks, especially DDoS attacks, can overwhelm systems, leading to errors and downtime. Even less aggressive bot activities can strain resources, causing operational issues. Here are the key indicators:</span></p>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":""},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzu396sk052c3575lrcwwgm4","clzu37nha010z0ha07vrr69dq"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Anomalies in Analytics and Metrics","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-54","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":16},{"lesson":3,"screen":17},{"lesson":3,"screen":18}],"leadHtml":"<p>Bots can skew analytics and performance metrics, which can misguide business decisions.</p>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":"","display":"match","pairs":[["Inflated page views and clicks","Ad impressions double while real user activity stays flat","Surges without matching legitimate activity may indicate bot interference."],["Discrepancies in conversion rates","Traffic climbs sharply but orders do not rise with it","High traffic with low conversion can point to bot activity."],["Inconsistent geographical data","A sudden influx of visits from regions you do not serve","Unexpected geographic patterns can suggest automated traffic."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"timeline","items":[{"title":"","date":"Identified Bot Signatures","description":"<p>Known patterns can help identify familiar automation, but can become stale or be copied. Use them with additional request and business evidence.</p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Repeated Access from Blocked IPs","description":"<p>Repeated activity after an address block may indicate evasion, a shared network, or rule configuration issues. Check which accounts and actions remain affected.</p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Suspicious User-Agent Strings","description":"<p>User-agent headers are self-declared and easily changed. A familiar browser name does not prove a human, and an unfamiliar value does not by itself prove abuse.</p>","image":null}],"source":["clzu3ki4i05463575ytnvgqdg","clzu3bi1b011z0ha01bw49n1u"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Detection of Known Bot Signature and IPs","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-55","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":19},{"lesson":3,"screen":20}],"display":"topics","leadHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Advanced security systems can identify known bot signatures or suspicious IP addresses associated with bot activities. Below are some of these key indicators:</span></p>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":""},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>Which pattern is the strongest sign of bot traffic rather than genuine demand?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>A spike that starts with a marketing email and brings higher sales volume.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Steady growth from new regions after launching local payment methods.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"2","title":"<p>A spike with no campaign, very fast requests per session, and flat sales.</p>","correct":true},{"id":"3","title":"<p>An overnight drop in traffic that recovers when business hours begin.</p>","correct":false}],"feedback":"<p>An unexplained spike with machine-speed sessions and no rise in sales points to automation. A campaign-driven spike with more sales, growth after a launch, and normal daily cycles all have clear business explanations.</p>","multi":false,"source":["clzu3x6x4016j0ha09sqq37ns","clzu3x6x4016i0ha0826bdo8s"],"lessonIndex":2,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-56","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":21}],"fixedOrder":true},{"kind":"activity","source":["clzu48u4805x4357529hmi8a9","clz02c5mt0aza3575y6r88qws","clw0sjc8c0amp3579n39cjekz"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) to Monitor Attacks","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-57","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":22},{"lesson":3,"screen":23},{"lesson":3,"screen":24}],"leadHtml":"<p>Measure abuse and customer experience together, against a baseline for each journey. Blocked-request counts alone do not show fraud prevented.</p>","display":"sort","cats":["Engagement and conversion","Accounts and access","Traffic and system health"],"items":[["Conversion rate",0,"Compare legitimate purchases with eligible sessions, and investigate causes before blaming bots."],["Bounce rate",0,"Unusually high bounces can suggest visits with no real engagement."],["Average session duration",0,"Many very short sessions can point to automated visits."],["Cart abandonment rate",0,"Separate normal browsing from repeated abusive reservations."],["Login success rate",1,"Track genuine logins, challenge abandonment, recovery, and confirmed compromise."],["New account creation rate",1,"A sudden rise in quiet, incomplete accounts may be bot-generated."],["Traffic source analysis",2,"Unexpected surges from one source with low engagement can indicate bots."],["Error rate",2,"Separate application failures from deliberate rate limits or blocks."]]},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>Product page traffic is up 40 percent, but conversion has fallen sharply. Which next step best tests whether bots are involved?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>Lower prices on the affected products to see whether conversion recovers.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Add more server capacity, since slow pages are the usual cause of this drop.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"2","title":"<p>Pause all marketing campaigns until the conversion rate returns to normal.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"3","title":"<p>Compare request speed, navigation paths, and device patterns for new traffic.</p>","correct":true}],"feedback":"<p>Rising traffic with falling conversion can mean bots are inflating visits. Comparing request speed, paths, and device patterns shows whether the new traffic behaves like people. Changing prices, adding capacity, or pausing campaigns acts before you know the cause.</p>","multi":false,"source":["clzu4ypzs01jx0e7ffb36fs3p","clzu4ypzs01jw0e7f23hnbp51"],"lessonIndex":2,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-58","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":25}],"fixedOrder":true},{"id":"BOT-SCENARIO-3","lessonIndex":2,"kind":"scenario","character":"assets/scenario-characters/lesson-3.png","title":"Scenario: When Traffic Looks Healthy","name":"Luis","role":"payments risk analyst","stages":[{"context":"You are Luis, a payments risk analyst. A campaign brings more visits, but checkout conversion falls. You also see many small payment attempts and a sharp increase in declines.","prompt":"What evidence do you examine next?","options":[{"text":"Correlate payment velocity, decline patterns, and session activity to investigate possible automated card testing during checkout.","good":true,"outcome":"Rapid attempts across multiple cards and repeated session patterns support a card-testing investigation. You separate suspicious payment behavior from the campaign-driven visitor increase."},{"text":"Use the visitor increase as proof of campaign success and review payment declines after the campaign ends.","good":false,"outcome":"Automated payment attempts continue while the team delays review. More visitors do not explain away repeated declines or show that customers are completing purchases."},{"text":"Assume all campaign visitors are attackers and block everyone arriving from the active campaign landing page.","good":false,"outcome":"Genuine campaign customers are blocked alongside suspicious sessions. The traffic source alone does not distinguish card testing from legitimate shopping."}]},{"context":"The investigation links the small declined attempts to automated sessions. Legitimate purchases still arrive from the campaign.","goodLead":"The combined evidence supports a targeted intervention.","badLead":"The payment and session review now provides a clearer basis for action.","prompt":"How will you judge the response?","options":[{"text":"Track total page views alone and declare success when the campaign returns to its usual visitor volume.","good":false,"outcome":"Page views do not show whether card testing stopped. The team could miss continuing abuse or damage to genuine checkout completion."},{"text":"Track blocked requests alone and declare success when the dashboard reports a larger number of blocked sessions.","good":false,"outcome":"More blocks may reflect either better protection or greater customer friction. Without payment and conversion measures, the team cannot judge the business outcome."},{"text":"Track abusive payment attempts, authorization outcomes, and genuine conversion to confirm protection without unnecessary customer friction.","good":true,"outcome":"Declining abusive attempts alongside stable legitimate conversion provides stronger evidence of recovery. The team can adjust controls if genuine customers begin to struggle."}]}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bot attacks are becoming increasingly sophisticated, making early detection crucial to protect digital infrastructure. Common indicators include sudden shifts in traffic, unusual behavior patterns, repeated failures, abnormal account or payment activity, and changes in customer experience metrics.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A spike in pageviews alone does not establish malicious automation. Review request velocity, unusual paths, repeated failures, device and network patterns, account creation rates, checkout completion, payment authorization outcomes, and business context such as marketing campaigns, product launches, and seasonal activity. Sophisticated automation can also occur without a dramatic traffic spike.</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-59.svg","alt":"Takeaways: watch traffic and behavior shifts, look for repeated failures, check account and payment patterns, compare with business context."},"caption":"","source":["cl2j5hw9g00061tea6pjfgm8s","clw1352q800kt357apoxk4mga","cl2l0b3fs00863a6uzv0mpb0x"],"lessonIndex":2,"title":"Summary","superHeadings":[],"nextHtml":"<p><strong><strong><strong><strong><span style=\"font-size: 20px; color: rgb(49, 53, 55);\"><strong>In the next lesson, we will learn about traditional bot management techniques.&nbsp;</strong></span></strong></strong></strong></strong></p>","id":"BOTQA-59","originalScreens":[{"lesson":3,"screen":26}],"layout":"conceptVisual"},{"kind":"intro","title":"Traditional Bot Management Techniques","objectiveTitle":"Learning Objectives","items":[{"title":"By the end of this lesson, learners will be able to:","date":"","description":"<ul><li><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Describe different types of traditional bot management techniques</span></p></li></ul>"}],"image":{"src":"assets/LearnUpon_Thumbnail_Templa-4.jpg","alt":""},"caption":"<p><strong>Lesson 4 of 8</strong></p>","source":["FbYMOwhj1qYjH2C1ND-LBTk1fbV3eRCu","nfepaPWNW1N2AKkKgj7WIAHzzp9eD80l","p687TuwZJFenxv5zyMsQTmNpCMC8bPrc"],"lessonIndex":3,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-60","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":1}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"8f103346-9a4b-4948-841d-86e7eaa2d8ad\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #000000; text-decoration: inherit\"><span>The rise of automated threats in eCommerce has posed significant challenges in fraud prevention. Traditional bot management techniques first evolved to combat these threats. Understanding these methods is helpful for professionals to understand their uses and limitations in safeguarding online transactions and customer data.</span></span></span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Let’s explore various traditional defenses against bot attacks, including CAPTCHAs, IP blocking, rate limiting, and more.&nbsp;</span></p></div>","source":["clw19xy7w0097357a5f0vd53w","iUgZFah4LCKvoq5tM0hcbsqThayscw_l"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Bot Management Techniques","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-61","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":2}],"layout":"lessonVisual","image":{"src":"assets/lesson-4-screen-2.png","alt":"An online storefront protected by layers of filtering, rate limits, and verification controls."}},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"process","items":[{"title":"Overview","description":"<p>CAPTCHAs are intended to help separate human use from automation. Visible tasks can impose accessibility and conversion costs and may be solved by automated tools or paid services. They are one response option, not a complete defense or a universal proof of humanity.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled design - 2024-08--3.png","alt":""},"isOverview":true},{"title":"Trigger Point Identification","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">CAPTCHAs are not applied universally; instead, they are triggered at specific points in the user journey where bot activity is most likely, such as during login attempts, form submissions, or checkout processes. </span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Identifying these points involves analyzing where bots typically target and where human verification is most needed to prevent abuse.</span></p>","image":null},{"title":"Challenge Presentation","date":"","description":"<p>Select a risk-appropriate challenge or alternative. Visual puzzles, managed checks, and risk scoring are different mechanisms and should not be treated as equally effective or equally accessible.</p>","image":null},{"title":"User Interaction","date":"","description":"<p>Provide a usable alternative for customers who cannot complete a challenge. Measure completion, abandonment, and support contacts. Atypical interaction or missing mouse input is not sufficient evidence of abuse.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Verification","date":"","description":"<p>Validate challenge results on the server using the provider protocol. Reject invalid, expired, or replayed tokens as required. Apply the intended hostname or action checks. Keep other fraud and rate controls in place after a challenge is passed.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Challenge Variation and Update","date":"","description":"<p>Test changes against abuse reduction and genuine-customer outcomes. Review accessibility and challenge failures by device and channel. Rotating puzzles alone does not solve an automated-abuse problem.</p>","image":null}],"source":["cm0bggel805yg3573mfwko2ro","clzvessqv0c9a3575wemhx79m","cm0zp66jj01vf3574p9noz4qg"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Captchas","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-62","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":3},{"lesson":4,"screen":4},{"lesson":4,"screen":5}],"display":"process"},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm0ct3fbd025j3573eq1oi06n","cm0clr2c4024q3573gqujnfe6"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Strengths and Limitations of CAPTCHAs","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-63","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":6}],"leadHtml":"<p>CAPTCHAs help in some situations and fall short in others.</p>","display":"sort","cats":["Strength","Limitation"],"items":[["Adds friction to some automated attempts",0,"A targeted challenge can slow some automation, depending on the implementation and journey."],["Can be triggered only at risky moments",0,"Challenges can be shown at selected trigger points instead of to every visitor."],["Paid solving services can pass the challenge",1,"Automated solvers and human farms reduce how well CAPTCHAs stop bots."],["Visual or audio tasks can exclude some customers",1,"Provide accessible alternatives and monitor abandonment."],["Replay and bypass paths can skip it entirely",1,"Validate results on the server and protect every path to the action."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"timeline","items":[{"title":"Overview","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #00AEEF; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>IP blocking</strong></span><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\"> is one of the most fundamental and widely used techniques for managing bot traffic. This method involves identifying IP addresses known to be associated with malicious activities and preventing them from accessing your systems. When a bot's IP address is recognized, it is added to a blocklist, ensuring that any future requests from that address are denied.&nbsp;</span></span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">Here is how IP blocking works:</span></span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled design - 2024-08--4.png","alt":""},"isOverview":true},{"title":"","date":"Identification","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">The first step is identifying IP addresses that are linked to malicious activities. This can be done using several sources, such as security logs, traffic analysis, and threat intelligence feeds that provide up-to-date lists of known malicious IP addresses.</span></span></p><p></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">Additionally, organizations may use automated systems that monitor traffic for suspicious patterns, such as an unusually high number of requests from a single IP, which could indicate bot activity.&nbsp;</span></span></p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Blocklist Addition","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">Once a malicious IP is identified, it is added to a blocklist. This blocklist is maintained by the network’s firewall or a web application firewall (WAF).</span></span></p><p></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">The blocklist is regularly updated as new threats emerge. In some cases, the blocklist may include entire IP ranges, particularly if attacks are originating from specific regions known for malicious activities.&nbsp;</span></span></p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Request Filtering ","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">When a request is made to the server, the firewall or WAF compares the incoming IP address against the blocklist. This check is performed in real time for every request. If the IP address matches one on the blocklist, the firewall prevents the request from proceeding.&nbsp;</span></span></p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Access Denial","description":"<p>An address rule prevents matching requests at its enforcement point; it does not stop the operator from using another address or an unprotected endpoint. Check shared-network effects and pair targeted blocking with other controls.</p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Continuous Monitoring","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">To maintain the effectiveness of IP blocking, continuous monitoring is essential. Security teams need to track emerging threats and update the blocklist regularly to reflect new malicious IPs.</span></span></p><p></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">Additionally, they must monitor traffic for false positives, where legitimate users might be inadvertently blocked. Tools like geolocation databases and threat intelligence services can help refine the blocklist to minimize disruption to legitimate users.&nbsp;</span></span></p>","image":null}],"source":["cm0cta2u2026v3573ru4xajin","clzvfwvbv09et0haafpnfhhvi","cm0zp78z800l20ddw7n162e0t"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"IP Blocking","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-64","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":7},{"lesson":4,"screen":8},{"lesson":4,"screen":9}],"display":"process"},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm0ctuup90a3s3573n7ucrcyu","cm0cttzl00a0m3573iqavgzjc"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Strengths and Limitations of IP Blocking","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-65","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":10}],"leadHtml":"<p>IP blocking is simple and fast, but it has clear limits.</p>","display":"sort","cats":["Strength","Limitation"],"items":[["Simple to set up and add to existing security tools",0,"IP blocking is easy to implement and integrates with firewalls and infrastructure."],["Gives immediate relief from known malicious sources",0,"It works well against low-sophistication bots using a few static addresses."],["Botnets spread attacks across many addresses",1,"Sophisticated attackers distribute activity so no single address stands out."],["Shared or dynamic addresses can block real customers",1,"Blocking an address can shut out genuine users behind it, costing revenue."]]},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>A merchant adds a CAPTCHA to login, but account takeover attempts continue at the same rate. What is the most likely reason?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>CAPTCHAs only check device fingerprints, which attackers can reset.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Attackers are using CAPTCHA-solving services to pass the challenge.</p>","correct":true},{"id":"2","title":"<p>CAPTCHAs only affect genuine users and never apply to automated logins.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"3","title":"<p>CAPTCHAs only work on payment pages and cannot protect a login page.</p>","correct":false}],"feedback":"<p>CAPTCHAs aim to tell humans from bots, but solving services and human farms can pass them at low cost. That is why CAPTCHAs work best as one layer alongside behavioral, account, and rate-based controls.</p>","multi":false,"source":["cm0cx0xs003180h8w9g4pbilm","cm0cx0xs003170h8w2hjrat43"],"lessonIndex":3,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-66","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":11}],"fixedOrder":true},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm0ctxxum00ds0dc72h2b0l7k","cm0ctxxum00dr0dc7gd6oc0cl","cm0zp7x1x01x10hew4hfp6686"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Rate Limiting","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-67","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":12},{"lesson":4,"screen":13},{"lesson":4,"screen":14}],"leadHtml":"<p>Rate limiting constrains activity over a time window at sensitive endpoints.</p>","display":"sequence","done":"Rate limits work best when they are set from expected behavior, enforced on a trusted server, logged, and tuned over time.","items":[["Set thresholds","Base limits on expected behavior and the cost of the protected action."],["Monitor requests","Count requests at the server or trusted edge, across browser, app, and API paths."],["Enforce the limit","Slow, delay, or block requests that exceed the threshold."],["Log and alert","Record each breach and alert the security team for analysis."],["Adjust over time","Compare abuse stopped with customer impact before changing limits."]]},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm0cu5wib0bgz3573169uf7ui","cm0ctxxum00du0dc7gq9u8j0f"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Strengths and Limitations of Rate Limiting","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-68","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":15}],"leadHtml":"<p>Rate limiting is effective, but it needs careful calibration.</p>","display":"sort","cats":["Strength","Limitation"],"items":[["Stops bots from overwhelming login, checkout, or API endpoints",0,"Limiting automated requests reduces server load and abuse."],["Can be tailored to different parts of the site",0,"Limits can differ for login pages, checkout, and APIs."],["Flags suspicious bursts that can trigger other controls",0,"Breaches can alert administrators or add security checks."],["Distributed attacks keep each address under the limit",1,"Botnets spread requests so no single source exceeds the threshold."],["Limits set too tight slow or block genuine customers",1,"Poor calibration harms the customer experience."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"timeline","items":[{"title":"Overview","description":"<p>A user-agent string describes a claimed browser or client. It is useful for context but can be forged. User-agent filtering should support other controls rather than decide whether an actor is human, authorized, or malicious.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (42).png","alt":""},"isOverview":true},{"title":"","date":"User Agent Analysis","description":"<p>Read the declared client as one request attribute. Compare it with other evidence where available. A browser name alone does not verify identity, and headless automation can be legitimate.</p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Pattern Matching","description":"<p>Known values may flag familiar tools, but attackers can change them. Avoid treating an outdated library name as conclusive proof of abuse.</p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"List Management","description":"<p>For permitted crawlers or agents, use operator verification where supported and narrow permissions by purpose, action, and endpoint. Do not build a universal allow rule from a self-declared name.</p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Filtering","description":"<p>Combine signals and enforce proportionate actions on a trusted server or edge. Test false positives and preserve access for approved services and legitimate users.</p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Logging and Review","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">All interactions filtered by user-agent are logged for further analysis. These logs help in identifying emerging threats and refining the filtering rules. Security teams review this data to adjust the lists and rules, ensuring that legitimate users are not mistakenly blocked while enhancing the system’s ability to detect new bot behaviors.&nbsp;</span></span></p>","image":null}],"source":["cm0cua51h0bki357392qwuivl","cm0cu826i008l0gar36oq035y","cm0zp8o5m00v50gbb3109ag1n"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"User-Agent Filtering","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-69","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":16},{"lesson":4,"screen":17},{"lesson":4,"screen":18}],"display":"process"},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm0cu81s200j10gd56ssr3yzf","cm0cu826i008o0garhfhp4dn3"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Strengths and Limitations of User-Agent Filtering","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-70","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":19}],"leadHtml":"<p>User-agent filtering is easy to apply, but easy to evade.</p>","display":"sort","cats":["Strength","Limitation"],"items":[["Easy to set up on most web servers",0,"User-agent filtering is a low-impact technique that most servers support."],["Filters bots that use generic user-agent strings",0,"Less sophisticated bots with obvious user agents are caught quickly."],["Attackers can copy a real browser's user agent",1,"The header is self-declared, so sophisticated bots simply imitate browsers."],["Cannot be relied on as the only control",1,"Use it as one layer alongside rate limiting, behavior, and other signals."]]},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm0cuqfgf002i0harebgn6hpj","cm0cuqfgf002h0har8y7eg64l","cm0zp9f8u00v60gbbfceghlzx"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Behavioral Analysis","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-71","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":20},{"lesson":4,"screen":21},{"lesson":4,"screen":22}],"leadHtml":"<p>Behavioral analysis looks at how visitors interact with a site to spot automation.</p>","display":"sequence","done":"Behavioral analysis collects only what it needs, compares behavior with normal patterns, responds in proportion, and keeps models under review.","items":[["Collect journey signals","Gather only necessary signals, such as action timing and navigation."],["Recognize patterns","Look for unusual sequences rather than one supposed human signature."],["Detect anomalies","Flag behavior such as impossibly fast navigation or perfectly regular timing."],["Trigger a response","Observe, limit, verify, or block according to confidence and harm."],["Update the models","Retrain with validated data and check false positives before expanding."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Strengths","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">Behavioral analysis is highly effective against sophisticated bots that can bypass traditional defenses. It provides a dynamic and adaptive approach to bot management, as it continuously learns and adjusts based on observed behaviors. This method also minimizes disruption to legitimate users, as it relies on subtle indicators rather than intrusive challenges.&nbsp;</span></span></p>","image":null},{"title":"Limitations","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">Behavioral analysis requires significant computational resources and sophisticated algorithms, making it more complex and costly to implement than traditional methods. </span></span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">Additionally, while it is effective against many types of bots, it may still struggle with advanced bots designed to mimic human behavior closely. As with other techniques, behavioral analysis is most effective when combined with a comprehensive security strategy.&nbsp;</span></span></p>","image":null}],"source":["cm0cuqexx00jj0gb44k051mz1","cm0cuqfgf002k0harhp8h2kr2"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Strengths and Limitations of Behavioral Analysis","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-72","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":23}],"display":"accordion"},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>A credential stuffing attack sends one login attempt per IP address from thousands of addresses. Why does a per-IP rate limit fail?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>Each address stays under the limit, so patterns must be seen across accounts.</p>","correct":true},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Rate limits only count checkout requests, so login attempts are not counted.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"2","title":"<p>Rate limits block whole countries, which attackers avoid with local addresses.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"3","title":"<p>Rate limits reset after each successful login, which attackers trigger first.</p>","correct":false}],"feedback":"<p>Rate limiting caps requests within a time window, but a distributed attack keeps each address under the limit. Detecting it needs account-level, device, and network patterns combined with authentication controls.</p>","multi":false,"source":["cm0cwx97600mc0haremwybvil","cm0cwx97600mb0har3pg54uwj"],"lessonIndex":3,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-73","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":24}],"fixedOrder":true},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">Beyond CAPTCHAs, IP blocking, and rate limiting, several other traditional defenses are involved in bot management.&nbsp;Here are examples of these other traditional defenses against bots:</span></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: #F5841F; text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>Deny Lists and Allow Lists</strong></span></span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Deny lists (blocklists) and allow lists are fundamental tools in bot management. Deny lists block traffic from known malicious sources by using data from threat intelligence feeds and security logs. These lists are integrated into firewalls or web application firewalls (WAFs) to prevent bots from accessing systems.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Allow lists, conversely, permit access only from trusted sources like business partners or internal users, ensuring secure access. Both lists require regular updates to remain effective, as attackers frequently change tactics.</span></p>","source":["cm0cwa9w80gr23573qfyewyzh","clw19xix7008t357ak3zz3rxw","DbyGRtUHE-KhR9Ni4eue1vxaEI_NGtKF"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Other Traditional Defenses","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-74","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":25},{"lesson":4,"screen":26}],"image":{"src":"assets/Untitled design - 2024-08--6.png","alt":""},"caption":""},{"kind":"image","html":"<p>Device fingerprinting combines attributes such as browser configuration and device or network signals to help link activity. It does not guarantee a unique device or establish who is using it. Shared environments, browser changes, and imitation limit certainty.</p><p>Use fingerprints as one input alongside account, request, and transaction evidence. Assess necessary collection, retention, and device-access requirements before deployment. Protect direct APIs and avoid blocking a customer solely because a fingerprint changes.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled design - 2024-08--8.png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["cm0cwkh220gsj35739t0nyzz5"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Other Traditional Defenses","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-75","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":27}]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"process","items":[{"title":"Overview","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">While traditional bot management techniques provide essential tools for combating bot attacks, they are not without limitations. Sophisticated bots can adapt to and bypass many of these defenses, necessitating a multi-layered approach to security. </span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Furthermore, implementing these measures can sometimes lead to false positives, where legitimate users are mistakenly identified as bots, impacting their experience. Therefore, it is crucial to continuously refine and adapt bot management strategies to stay ahead of emerging threats.</span></p><p style=\"text-align: center\"><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">These additional challenges include:</span></p>","image":null,"isOverview":true},{"title":"Solver Services and Cybercrime-as-a-Service","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">These services have made it easy for attackers to bypass traditional defenses. With cheap and readily available solver services, attackers can quickly overcome CAPTCHAs and other simple verification systems. </span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Cybercrime-as-a-service is a well-established market that provides all-in-one solutions for defeating a site's security, making traditional defenses less reliable.</span></p>","image":null},{"title":"High Maintenance of Defenses","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Keeping defenses updated with new rules and emerging threats is labor-intensive and prone to errors. Failure to stay current can leave systems vulnerable, while over-complicating rules may inadvertently lead to false positives or performance degradation.</span></p>","image":null}],"source":["cm0zpfgdr02pv3574g93rnuqc","cm0zpdcth02p23574065g6fiz","clz0ff58x0ka23575o40t3ji3"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Challenges and Limitations","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-76","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":28},{"lesson":4,"screen":29}],"display":"topics"},{"id":"BOT-SCENARIO-4","lessonIndex":3,"kind":"scenario","character":"assets/scenario-characters/lesson-4.png","title":"Scenario: A Rule That Blocks Shoppers","name":"Ravi","role":"security operations lead","stages":[{"context":"You are Ravi, the security operations lead. Repeated login requests are increasing. A proposed rule would block an entire shared network also used by many genuine customers.","prompt":"How do you refine the rule?","options":[{"text":"Apply the network block to everyone and use the total blocked-request count as the only success measure.","good":false,"outcome":"Customers sharing the network lose access. Counting blocked requests alone hides the effect on genuine logins and does not show whether distributed abuse continues."},{"text":"Use targeted rate limits and other signals, then monitor successful logins and customer friction before expanding enforcement.","good":true,"outcome":"The team slows abusive login activity while checking effects on genuine users. Combining signals reduces reliance on a network address that many customers share."},{"text":"Trust the browser user-agent string and allow every request that claims to come from a familiar browser.","good":false,"outcome":"Attackers can imitate familiar user-agent strings. Allowing those requests without other checks leaves the repeated login behavior largely unchanged."}]},{"context":"Attackers begin spreading requests across more addresses. Customer support also reports difficulty completing a visual challenge.","goodLead":"Your monitoring reveals both changing attack behavior and customer friction.","badLead":"The team revisits its controls as the limits of the first response become clear.","prompt":"What do you change?","options":[{"text":"Layer account and device signals with rate controls, and provide an accessible alternative to the visual challenge.","good":true,"outcome":"Layered controls address activity across rotating addresses. An accessible alternative helps genuine users complete verification while the team monitors whether abuse and friction decline."},{"text":"Add more addresses to the block list, retain the visual challenge, and stop monitoring successful customer logins.","good":false,"outcome":"Address rotation continues and some genuine users still struggle. An expanding list alone cannot resolve distributed behavior or reveal accessibility problems."},{"text":"Remove every login safeguard immediately and rely on customer complaints to identify the next wave of automated requests.","good":false,"outcome":"The login flow becomes easier to abuse, and complaints arrive after harm occurs. The team needs proportionate controls and ongoing monitoring rather than removing protection."}]}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Traditional bot management techniques, such as CAPTCHAs, IP restrictions, deny or allow lists, device signals, and rate limiting, still play an important role in eCommerce defenses.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">These controls remain useful, but none is a complete safeguard on its own. Distributed actors may rotate IP addresses, CAPTCHA solvers can defeat challenges, and device fingerprints are probabilistic rather than guaranteed identities. Rate limits should be tailored to the endpoint, account, device, network, and business context, with ongoing monitoring for legitimate-customer friction.</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-77.svg","alt":"Takeaways: traditional controls still help, none is complete on its own, attackers rotate addresses and solve challenges, tailor and monitor each control."},"caption":"","source":["Ycv1FDSJ4o4GnaOMsAir5KXZbcjTyKGN","cm0zpc4wp02nh3574ikl755il","gsy6eUYjA1qB31FNrZRf8Y-4NFq3JZaD"],"lessonIndex":3,"title":"Summary","superHeadings":[],"nextHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 2rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(49, 53, 55); text-decoration: inherit\"><strong>In the next lesson, we will learn about the emerging bot management solutions.</strong></span></span></p>","id":"BOTQA-77","originalScreens":[{"lesson":4,"screen":30}],"layout":"conceptVisual"},{"kind":"intro","title":"Current Bot Management Solutions","objectiveTitle":"Learning Objectives","items":[{"title":"By the end of this lesson, learners will be able to:","date":"","description":"<ul><li><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Explain the emerging bot management solutions being utilized to protect eCommerce platforms</span></p></li></ul>"}],"image":{"src":"assets/LearnUpon_Thumbnail_Templa-5.jpg","alt":""},"caption":"<p><strong>Lesson 5 of 8</strong></p>","source":["cl2u7wtl900033a6nxgkucbtb","cl2i6hq2f006f3a6j5f3zfgqz","cl298xpn600063a6j0jis29ek"],"lessonIndex":4,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-78","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":1}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<p>Current bot defenses combine request, behavior, account, and transaction evidence. Rules, machine learning, and behavioral analysis can complement one another; no technique guarantees accurate classification.</p><p>Choose controls for the affected journey, including browser, mobile-app, and API traffic. Evaluate effectiveness using missed abuse, legitimate-customer friction, and business outcomes.</p><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">In practice</p><ul><li>Protect browser, mobile-app, and API traffic, not just the website.</li><li>Combine request, behavior, account, and transaction evidence.</li><li>Judge success by abuse stopped and genuine customers not blocked.</li></ul></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (43).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["cm2nqwsdn08ka3579245sg5h3","cl5czzydq0059356qqon9j57c","clz1fgd49001l0g9lctfx7j10"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Current Bot Management Solutions","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-79","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":2},{"lesson":5,"screen":3}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<p>Attackers may imitate or alter client telemetry. Server-observed requests and business outcomes provide other evidence, but requests still contain attacker-controlled inputs.</p><p>Validate signal integrity and combine views. Obfuscation may increase attacker effort, but it is not a substitute for trusted validation or enforcement.</p><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">In practice</p><ul><li>Check that client signals have not been altered before acting on them.</li><li>Compare client signals with what your servers observe.</li><li>Treat obfuscation as extra effort for attackers, not as protection.</li></ul></div>","source":["cm2nqwsdn08ka3579245sg5h3","cl5czzydq0059356qqon9j57c","clz1fgd49001l0g9lctfx7j10"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Data Authenticity and Detection","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-79-continued","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":2},{"lesson":5,"screen":3}],"layout":"conceptVisual","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-79-continued.svg","alt":"Client-side signals: describe browser or app behavior, can be altered or imitated, must be validated. Server-side evidence: requests and business outcomes, harder to fake but still contain attacker inputs, combine both views."}},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm2nr0y0p08ob3579vdcs4o74","clz1ghiby04s00g807ck68h2c"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Client and Server-Side Bot Detection","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-80","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":4}],"leadHtml":"<p>Client signals can be missing or faked, and server and edge evidence can be combined with them. Validate across layers and enforce decisions on a trusted server.</p>","display":"sort","cats":["Client-side signal","Server or edge signal"],"items":[["Mouse movement captured by a browser script",0,"Browser scripts collect interaction signals, which can be imitated."],["Device details reported by a mobile app SDK",0,"App SDKs report device signals that can be missing or altered."],["Interaction timing measured on the page",0,"Timing on the page is client evidence, not proof of a human."],["Request rate across many accounts seen at the edge",1,"Edge services see patterns across accounts and networks."],["Direct API calls that never load the web page",1,"Only the server sees calls that skip the browser entirely."],["Payment outcomes linked to the same network",1,"Servers can connect transaction results to request patterns."]]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"f27d08e4-4987-4fdf-9c9e-3784b1f2ab92\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Advanced machine learning has become a cornerstone in bot detection and mitigation strategies. By leveraging sophisticated algorithms and vast datasets, these models can identify patterns, anomalies, and new threats with remarkable accuracy. As bot tactics continue to evolve, machine learning provides a dynamic solution that adapts to emerging threats, offering a powerful and scalable defense against increasingly complex bot attacks.</span></p></div>","source":["cm2nrerzc08pk35794ivby5j1","cm2nqw98208ir3579259uynok"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Advanced Machine Learning","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-81","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":5}],"layout":"lessonVisual","image":{"src":"assets/lesson-5-screen-5.png","alt":"Human and bot behavior patterns being analyzed to identify suspicious activity."}},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm0e08rer0j3l3573m1hn4y9f","clz1gkwpx0xgf35757rmqkdhs","cm2nqyw2v08md3579imsjemdy"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Machine Learning Algorithms and Techniques","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-82","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":6},{"lesson":5,"screen":7}],"leadHtml":"<p>Machine learning models learn from data to separate bots from genuine users.</p>","display":"match","pairs":[["Supervised learning","Learns from labeled examples of known bot and human traffic","Supervised models perform well on known bot patterns when labels are good."],["Unsupervised learning","Groups similar behavior to find new patterns without labels","Clustering can surface previously unknown bot activity."],["Reinforcement learning","Improves through trial, reward, and penalty as tactics change","Reinforcement learning adapts as attacker strategies shift."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"tabs","items":[{"title":"Behavioral Data Collection","date":"","description":"<p>Collect behavior relevant to the protected action and document why it is needed. Do not assume more telemetry means better detection. Minimize sensitive data and assess collection requirements with privacy teams.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-27.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Network Traffic Analysis","date":"","description":"<p>Network and request attributes can reveal shared infrastructure or patterns across sessions. Residential proxies and shared networks complicate interpretation. Combine network signals with account and transaction evidence.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-30.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Feature Engineering","date":"","description":"<p>Select features that help answer a defined abuse question. Check missing data, labeling errors, and leakage from the outcome being predicted. Test performance on representative browser, app, and API traffic.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-25.png","alt":""}}],"source":["cm0e3ax5z0jum3573r7vtqroo","cm0e33rm80jtt3573ejr35nkz","cm0e0ukex0jrz35735e3hjc6t"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Data Collection and Feature Engineering","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-83","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":8},{"lesson":5,"screen":9}],"display":"topics","leadHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); text-decoration: inherit\">Accurate bot detection hinges on the quality and comprehensiveness of the data used to train machine-learning models. Collecting behavioral data, analyzing network traffic, and engineering relevant features are essential steps in creating robust datasets.&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></span></p>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":""},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm0fdctod0kn23573t71of2xn","cm0fdb4d80km73573bij6dwlk","cm0e4p6na0kjw3573qxih4umd"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Anomaly Detection and Pattern Recognition","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-84","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":10},{"lesson":5,"screen":11},{"lesson":5,"screen":12}],"leadHtml":"<p>Detecting bots means finding behavior that departs from the norm, not just matching known patterns.</p>","display":"imghot","image":{"src":"assets/lesson-5-screen-5.png","alt":"Dashboard comparing a smooth human pattern with a regular bot pattern under a magnifying glass"},"pins":[{"x":61,"y":33,"title":"Statistical anomaly detection","text":"Statistical methods such as z-scores, moving averages, and control charts set normal ranges, then flag large deviations such as sudden spikes in login attempts."},{"x":47,"y":60,"title":"Time-series analysis","text":"Examining activity over time reveals trends, seasonal variation, and outliers, such as bots that run at fixed intervals or specific times."},{"x":15,"y":58,"title":"Behavioral clustering","text":"Clustering algorithms group sessions with similar behavior, which separates human users from bots and makes outliers easier to spot."}]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Overview","description":"<p>Attack patterns and legitimate behavior change. Review whether models still support the intended business outcomes. Controlled updates, representative tests, and rollback plans matter more than a claim that a model learns continuously.</p>","image":null,"isOverview":true},{"title":"Continuous Learning","date":"","description":"<p>Monitor false positives, missed attacks, and changes in data or score distributions. Investigate whether a change reflects an attack, a product release, a campaign, or missing telemetry before retraining.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-32.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Model Retraining","date":"","description":"<p>Train and evaluate on appropriate, reviewed data. Separate evaluation data from training where practical. Compare against the existing version, use a limited rollout, and retain a rollback route.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-29.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Feedback Loops","date":"","description":"<p>Use confirmed incidents, customer complaints, and analyst reviews to improve labels. Guard against malicious or mistaken feedback. Record the change, owner, test results, and customer impact.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-34.png","alt":""}}],"source":["cm0fdskqk0krl3573acf7jt1s","cm0fdp35a0kpy35732vl9815n","cm0fdhxe40ko43573guqq9fyu"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Adaptive Learning and Model Updating","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-85","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":13},{"lesson":5,"screen":14}],"display":"topics"},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>A team trains a supervised model on labeled examples of past bot attacks. What is its main strength and main weakness?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>Strong on brand-new attacks, but it cannot measure its accuracy without labels.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Strong on every attack type, but it slows checkout as it only runs in browsers.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"2","title":"<p>Strong on human traffic, but it cannot use behavioral data such as mouse moves.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"3","title":"<p>Strong on known patterns, but it can miss new tactics missing from its labels.</p>","correct":true}],"feedback":"<p>Supervised learning learns from labeled examples, so it performs well on known bot behaviors when labels are good. It can miss new tactics that look different from its training data, so it needs monitoring, retraining, and other detection methods alongside it.</p>","multi":false,"source":["clhzd3kq800g9356r9d1bwldy","clp32mx5h01fa356xv48i0mv8"],"lessonIndex":4,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-86","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":15}],"fixedOrder":true},{"kind":"image","html":"<p>Fingerprinting combines device, network, or behavioral attributes to help assess activity. It is probabilistic: environments can be shared, attributes can change, and attackers can imitate them. A fingerprint is not a guaranteed identity.</p><p>Challenges and attestation provide different kinds of evidence. A result must be validated and interpreted within its scope. Passing a check does not establish transaction authorization or eliminate fraud risk.</p>","source":["clw2n2i250axv35730jdbvz1t","clp1rdex100cp356xzwvnopf8"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Fingerprint Technologies and Challenge Response Tests","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-87","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":16}],"layout":"conceptVisual","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-87.svg","alt":"Fingerprinting: combines device, network, and behavior attributes, probabilistic, can be imitated, not a guaranteed identity. Challenges and attestation: test a specific claim, must be validated, limited scope, passing does not authorize a payment."}},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"tabs","items":[{"title":"Overview","description":"<p>Behavioral signals can help identify patterns, but they do not reliably identify every person or bot. Evaluate performance across devices and accessibility needs and use other evidence before enforcement.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled design - 2024-08--10.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Mouse and Touch","description":"<p>Movement and touch patterns provide context on some interfaces. Automated tools can imitate them; genuine users may use keyboard navigation or assistive devices. Missing mouse movement is not proof of a bot.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-31.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Typing Signals","description":"<p>Timing between events may be informative, but password managers, autofill, and assistive tools change typing patterns. Do not capture secret values or assume web pages can reliably measure key pressure.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-33.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Journey Sequences","description":"<p>Link action sequences with their business outcomes. Repeated sensitive actions across accounts can matter more than human-looking movement. Check false positives and collect only the signals needed for the purpose.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) (3-26.png","alt":""}}],"source":["cm0fez6210lwt35739c8kgaea","cm0fev7050lv03573ppv7rbs6","cm0fetys90lqy3573f5c1go2g"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Behavioral Fingerprinting","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-88","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":17},{"lesson":5,"screen":18}],"display":"topics"},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm2nrjnh408sg35797mfh2d57","cm2kzm2aa03sz35790v18dy5e","cm2kzlczl03qk3579jig9gstj"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Challenge-Response Tests","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-89","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":19},{"lesson":5,"screen":20}],"leadHtml":"<p>Challenges are selected checks within a layered response. They may add attacker cost but can also add customer friction, so match the check to the risk and provide usable alternatives.</p>","display":"match","pairs":[["Visible tasks","Can be solved by paid services and can exclude some people","Measure completion and abandonment rather than assuming every human can pass."],["Managed checks","Results must be validated on the server using the provider protocol","Managed checks and risk-scoring services are different products with different results."],["Attestation and authentication","Supports a limited claim, not proof that every action is authorized","Passkeys verify control of an authenticator, not that a transaction is legitimate."],["Invisible checks","Reduce interruption but still need privacy and accessibility review","Invisible does not automatically mean accessible or privacy-preserving."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"process","items":[{"title":"Layered Detection","description":"<p>Compare edge, application, account, and transaction evidence. Different layers see different activity, and client telemetry can be unavailable. Include direct API paths.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Layered Enforcement","description":"<p>Use controls at the sensitive action: validated sessions, limits, inventory rules, payment checks, or verification. Prevent bypass around the user interface and keep a recovery route for genuine users.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Monitoring and Rollback","description":"<p>Monitor customer completion, confirmed abuse, added latency, and support contacts. Assign an owner and test whether a new rule is causing friction; narrow or roll it back when necessary.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Responsible Sharing","description":"<p>Share relevant indicators under documented agreements and access controls. Do not circulate raw personal data or device fingerprints by default. Define purpose, retention, recipients, and legal requirements.</p>","image":null}],"source":["cm2kzsvve03uo3579rjjafxvl","cm2nrhu2708rg3579cxfekebs","cm2nrhaxx08qx3579lwh0fars"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Multi-Layered Security Approach","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-90","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":21},{"lesson":5,"screen":22}],"display":"topics","leadHtml":"<p>Combine complementary evidence with controls at the protected action. Test both abuse reduction and the effect on legitimate customers.</p>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":""},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm2l091cu04k735797rb7fda7","cm2l086g504iw3579p80s0j5y","cm2l054qu04fl3579le7zs0l4"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Future Trends and Innovations in Fingerprinting","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-91","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":23},{"lesson":5,"screen":24},{"lesson":5,"screen":25}],"leadHtml":"<p>As bots become more sophisticated, detection technology must evolve too. Treat each new technology as a hypothesis to test, with evidence of outcomes, privacy impact, and cost before adoption.</p>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":"","display":"match","pairs":[["Purpose-based agent access","Separating search, training, browsing, and transactions, then scoping each","Recognizing an agent does not authorize every end user or request."],["Privacy-aware signals","Using limited, necessary telemetry with set retention periods","Collecting less data requires careful signal selection and validation."],["Managed and invisible challenges","Reducing visible tasks while keeping server-side validation and fallbacks","Accessibility and other controls still need to be maintained."],["Network and protocol evidence","Fingerprints that do not depend entirely on browser behavior","Shared stacks and spoofing limit certainty, so add account context."],["Governed models","Staged releases, reviewed feedback, and rollback for detection models","More automation in model operations does not guarantee better protection."]]},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>Which approach best supports bot defense while reducing the personal data a merchant collects?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>Collecting as many device attributes as possible so every visitor is identified.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Invisible, risk-based checks that use fewer, minimized signals with clear limits.</p>","correct":true},{"id":"2","title":"<p>Showing a hard CAPTCHA to every visitor so no behavioral data is ever needed.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"3","title":"<p>Recording full session replays of every visitor so analysts can review later.</p>","correct":false}],"feedback":"<p>Invisible, risk-based checks with minimized signals support defense while limiting data collection. Collecting every attribute or recording full sessions gathers far more data than needed, and challenging every visitor harms genuine customers while solvers still get through.</p>","multi":false,"source":["clhzdh3by00ic356rs28xq0d2","clhzdguci00ib356rdabfow7w"],"lessonIndex":4,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-92","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":26}],"fixedOrder":true},{"id":"BOT-SCENARIO-5","lessonIndex":4,"kind":"scenario","character":"assets/scenario-characters/lesson-5.png","title":"Scenario: A Model Under Pressure","name":"Maya","role":"bot detection specialist","stages":[{"context":"You are Maya, a bot detection specialist. Browser telemetry looks human, yet server logs show repeated automated checkout requests. A colleague recommends trusting the client-side score alone.","prompt":"What do you do with the conflicting signals?","options":[{"text":"Allow every request with a human-looking client score and discard conflicting server evidence from the model review.","good":false,"outcome":"Automated checkout requests continue. Client-generated signals can be imitated or altered, so discarding server evidence leaves the team exposed to manipulated inputs."},{"text":"Block every session that moves a mouse and assume all browser interaction signals have become completely unreliable.","good":false,"outcome":"Genuine shoppers are blocked too. One suspicious pattern does not make all interaction data useless; the team needs to validate and combine evidence."},{"text":"Validate the client telemetry and combine request, behavior, and transaction evidence before adjusting the detection and enforcement.","good":true,"outcome":"The team identifies inconsistent telemetry alongside repeated checkout behavior. Combining validated signals gives a stronger basis for deciding which activity to challenge or block."}]},{"context":"After a model update, support reports more genuine customers being challenged. Attack methods have also changed since training.","goodLead":"Your combined evidence creates a useful baseline for reviewing the update.","badLead":"The team restores a broader evidence set before reviewing the update.","prompt":"How do you evaluate the model?","options":[{"text":"Compare the number of automated blocks only and keep the update whenever that number rises after deployment.","good":false,"outcome":"A higher block count can include genuine customers. The team could retain a harmful update while missing false positives, drift, or attacks that still pass through."},{"text":"Review labels, signal quality, drift, and false positives, then tune the model against verified customer and attack outcomes.","good":true,"outcome":"The team finds where the model is misclassifying activity and adjusts it using verified outcomes. Continued monitoring checks both missed abuse and unnecessary customer challenges."},{"text":"Assume the original training data remains representative and increase challenges for every visitor without further model validation.","good":false,"outcome":"Universal challenges increase friction without testing the model problem. Changing behavior and data quality require evaluation rather than confidence in an old training set."}]}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"e7cd48c7-018f-4b1c-9e1a-8ae7d0fee878\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">As bot attacks become more advanced, organizations increasingly rely on layered detection and response rather than any single signal or control. Current solutions combine behavior analysis, machine learning, request evaluation, and enforcement across the customer journey.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Client-side telemetry can describe browser or app behavior, but data generated on a client may be altered or imitated. Server-side and edge defenses can also respond in real time when the architecture supports it. Effective programs layer request, behavior, identity, transaction, and verified client signals, and they evaluate models for signal quality, drift, false positives, false negatives, and adversarial manipulation.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-93.svg","alt":"Takeaways: layer signals and controls, client data can be faked, use server and edge responses, monitor models over time."},"caption":"","source":["cl2r6iy0z01if1u81gbls8q58","clh6muev700rn356pfl05clgr","clgoevjnb0178356y64tfho6o"],"lessonIndex":4,"title":"Summary","superHeadings":[],"nextHtml":"<div data-editor-id=\"7ab67e5e-0d59-49da-b527-1ffc66c4eaef\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 2rem\"><strong>In the next lesson, we will learn about strategic considerations for bot management.</strong></span></p></div>","id":"BOTQA-93","originalScreens":[{"lesson":5,"screen":27}],"layout":"conceptVisual"},{"kind":"intro","title":"Strategic Considerations for Bot Management","objectiveTitle":"Learning Objectives","items":[{"title":"By the end of this lesson, learners will be able to:","date":"","description":"<ul><li><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Identify the technologies and solutions necessary to combat and manage bots</span></p></li></ul>"}],"image":{"src":"assets/LearnUpon_Thumbnail_Templa-6.jpg","alt":""},"caption":"<div data-editor-id=\"d8c69e30-bbd4-42c1-a088-96bfa0bd36a7\"><p><strong>Lesson 6 of 8</strong></p></div>","source":["tfVeNtguVd00ohkG7YTPgfN375YPVdKR","peS5DHXuyrhLMAC5qO5Gc9ADcmA56NsO","iCrWhwP1f9SbJ-XAvvm_gGJ6MOpsf58Z"],"lessonIndex":5,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-94","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":1}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"380b0982-7896-4979-b32e-f8cb48e7f6fe\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">As bot attacks become increasingly prevalent and sophisticated, it is essential for businesses to adopt a strategic approach to bot management. This involves not only understanding the technical aspects of detection and mitigation but also assessing the specific risks and impacts these attacks may have on your business. Additionally, legal and ethical considerations play a crucial role in shaping an effective bot management strategy.</span></p></div><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">Questions to ask</p><ul><li>Which customer journeys and assets would hurt most if attacked?</li><li>Which bot threats are most likely for your business and industry?</li><li>Which legal, privacy, and customer experience limits apply to your response?</li></ul></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (45).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["cm0zpw9c8044t3574c70gmex2","-FDrGJOeVoOwXsTd53_m-RtHkTd06JKm"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Strategic Considerations for Bot Management","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-95","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":2}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"11731280-9205-4936-aab9-278b8b567d5c\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Understanding the unique risks and potential impacts of bot attacks on your business is the first step in developing an effective bot management strategy. This involves a thorough analysis of your business's digital environment, assets, and the specific types of bot threats you may face.</span></p></div>","source":["cm0zq81lg045o3574eh0gpd7k","tbRfsZb64I4ZLG-0wJjzGkVFY0RrO2si"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Assessing the Risk and Impact of Bot Attacks","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-96","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":3}],"layout":"lessonVisual","image":{"src":"assets/lesson-6-screen-3.png","alt":"An analyst reviewing risk indicators for accounts, payment systems, and inventory."}},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm2mdvbeu03h435796h92zm8b","cm2mds3tj02i43579gqe2viu2"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Identifying Business Assets and Vulnerabilities","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-97","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":4},{"lesson":6,"screen":5}],"leadHtml":"<p>Identify critical digital assets and assess vulnerabilities to understand where bots might strike. Bots can also pollute customer data sets that drive analytics and marketing decisions.</p>","display":"sequence","done":"Start with a complete inventory, test it for weak points, then focus resources on the assets that matter most.","items":[["Build an asset inventory","List all digital assets and categorize them by importance to the business."],["Assess vulnerabilities","Use scanners, manual testing, and third-party audits to find weak points."],["Prioritize risks","Use a risk matrix to rank assets by value and the impact of compromise."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"tabs","items":[{"title":"Overview","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"7a02d554-02c6-4cdd-b460-36cde645c3b6\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Different types of bots pose various threats, including credential stuffing, scraping, and automated fraud. Evaluate the likelihood and potential impact of these threats on your business. Assess the various bot attack vectors that could target your business, considering both common and emerging threats.</span></p></div>","image":null,"isOverview":true},{"title":"Threat Modeling","date":"","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"6062c189-982d-41a8-b349-caec677ab8c8\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Develop threat models to map out potential bot attack scenarios. Identify the methods bots might use to infiltrate your systems, such as automated login attempts, web scraping, and form submission abuse.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --20.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Scenario Analysis","date":"","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"4ebf0bc9-ffc0-4c12-aff3-254f480bbe23\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Conduct scenario analysis to understand how different bot attacks could affect your business. Evaluate the likelihood and potential impact of each scenario.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --22.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Historical Data Review","date":"","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"e8981e10-1b97-4371-8068-9a3645f9fea7\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Review historical data and incident reports to identify patterns and trends in bot attacks. This can provide insights into the types of threats most likely to target your business.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --23.png","alt":""}}],"source":["cm2mdy4yr03jg35796pb93470","cm2mdx76g03hz3579ykpn2q0f"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Evaluating Potential Bot Threats","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-98","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":6},{"lesson":6,"screen":7}],"display":"topics"},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm2me1r9703nb3579mtp3kga8","cm2me0gf103mn357989k6m9ir","cm2mdztpy03k93579rmthkrqg"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Qualifying the Impact of Bot Attacks","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-99","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":8},{"lesson":6,"screen":9}],"leadHtml":"<p>Assess the financial, operational, and reputational impacts of bot attacks to prioritize mitigation and resources. Then use metrics such as transaction volumes, churn rates, and response times to estimate the cost.</p>","display":"sort","cats":["Financial","Operational","Reputational"],"items":[["Fraud losses and chargebacks",0,"Direct costs that hit the bottom line."],["Higher cloud and per-request platform costs",0,"Bot traffic inflates infrastructure and third-party fees."],["Increased server load and slower pages",1,"Bots degrade site performance for genuine customers."],["Staff time spent handling incidents",1,"Response work pulls teams away from planned tasks."],["Customer churn after account takeovers",2,"Customers leave when they feel their accounts are unsafe."],["Negative publicity about a breach",2,"Public attention damages brand trust and competitiveness."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"timeline","items":[{"title":"","date":"Risk Mitigation Strategies","description":"<p>A risk plan connects the abused journey, likely harm, evidence, response, owner, and success measures. Assign fraud, payments, security, engineering, support, and privacy responsibilities as appropriate. Include incident escalation and a rollback route.</p>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Incident Response Plan","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"2c2be0f1-1b91-48f5-bc89-8d8208d77c30\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Establish a response plan for addressing bot attacks promptly and effectively. Define roles and responsibilities, communication protocols, and escalation procedures.</span></p></div>","image":null},{"title":"","date":"Regular Review and Update","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"c380cfd8-81ca-4bd6-8537-fb7c4beb7ec3\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Continuously review and update your risk management plan to account for new threats and changes in your business environment. Conduct regular risk assessments and adjust your strategies as needed.</span></p></div>","image":null}],"source":["cm2ne7inh047y3579mfphplo4","cm2ne3psm045j3579xrl3k3ns"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Developing a Risk Management Plan","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-100","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":10},{"lesson":6,"screen":11}],"display":"topics","leadHtml":"<div data-editor-id=\"d6ae61bf-73d9-474d-9445-1128a27d4094\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">A comprehensive risk management plan outlines the strategies and measures to mitigate the identified risks and minimize the impact of bot attacks. Create a detailed plan that addresses the specific risks and impacts identified in your assessment.</span></p></div>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":""},{"kind":"image","html":"<p>Bot management must protect customers while respecting privacy, accessibility, and other applicable requirements. Work with legal and privacy teams to identify the lawful basis for processing and any separate rules for device storage or access.</p><p>Do not assume explicit consent is always the correct basis, or that fraud prevention automatically removes consent requirements. Document purpose, necessity, minimization, retention, access, and vendor responsibilities.</p><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">In practice</p><ul><li>Involve legal and privacy teams before deploying new detection tools.</li><li>Collect only the signals you need, and set clear retention periods.</li><li>Make sure customers who are wrongly challenged have a way to get help.</li></ul></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (47).png","alt":""},"caption":"","source":["cm2neecfa05xa357962783nbm","eH2hN-7O2O8KMPaSYGqT-5SAp1Qgd2oD"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Legal and Ethical Considerations","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-101","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":12}]},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm2nenscf060d357923c2xizk","cm2neezcq05xy3579f86cvby0"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Understanding Regulatory Requirements","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-102","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":13},{"lesson":6,"screen":14}],"leadHtml":"<p>Identify applicable requirements by jurisdiction, data, customer group, and processing purpose. A vendor badge is not a merchant compliance determination.</p>","display":"match","pairs":[["Compliance and audit","Document the lawful basis for processing and review actual data flows","For payment integrations, determine scope with the payments and security teams."],["Policy development","Create data protection, incident response, and user privacy policies","Policies turn regulatory standards into day-to-day practice."],["Regular updates","Follow regulatory changes through industry forums and legal advice","Requirements change, so compliance strategies need regular review."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"tabs","items":[{"title":"Overview","description":"<p>Personal data used for bot defense needs a defined purpose, lawful basis, protection, and retention period. Assess actual data flows and device-access requirements with privacy and legal teams.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Data Minimization","description":"<p>Collect what is necessary for a defined abuse question. Limit collection to relevant journeys and avoid secrets or unrelated personal information.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --24.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Protection and Retention","description":"<p>Encryption protects data from unauthorized access. Pseudonymization reduces exposure but does not automatically make information anonymous. Assess re-identification risk and define deletion and retention.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --30.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Lawful Basis and Device Access","description":"<p>Choose the appropriate lawful basis for processing and obtain consent where required. Separately assess device storage or access rules and valid exceptions; fraud prevention is not a blanket exemption.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --26.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Access and Vendor Controls","description":"<p>Restrict access to necessary staff and services, protect transfers, and document vendor responsibilities. Apply retention and deletion. Exclude passwords and complete payment credentials from investigation logs.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --28.png","alt":""}}],"source":["cm2neu8eq062q3579uqj2epwn","cm2nesyv506193579ckymx6up"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Privacy and Data Protection","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-103","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":15},{"lesson":6,"screen":16}],"display":"topics"},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm2nf314n066x3579ij9ox6pw","cm2nf1lkx065z3579n07pt05c","cm2newwno063l3579va7jslyj"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Ethical Considerations","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-104","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":17},{"lesson":6,"screen":18}],"leadHtml":"<p>Evaluate how a control affects genuine users as well as fraud losses.</p>","display":"match","pairs":[["Transparency","Explaining in plain notices what data bot checks use","Accessible notices explain processing and rights without overpromising."],["Fairness","Testing controls across devices, networks, and accessibility needs","Testing reduces false positives for customers with atypical setups."],["User trust","Engaging customers about their concerns with security checks","Openness about bot management builds and keeps trust."],["Accountable oversight","Assigning review across legal, privacy, fraud, and product teams","Shared oversight keeps controls proportionate and accountable."]]},{"kind":"activity","source":["cm2nfa1c706w83579mmyhfkuv","cm2nf8rwq06ua3579lt72mr4v"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Legal Implications of Bot Mitigation","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-105","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":19},{"lesson":6,"screen":20},{"lesson":6,"screen":21}],"leadHtml":"<p>Bot mitigation techniques, such as challenge-response tests and traffic filtering, can have legal implications. Make sure these practices do not violate user rights.</p>","display":"match","pairs":[["Legal counsel","Have experts review bot management policies against applicable laws","Counsel confirms that practices comply with laws and regulations."],["Documentation and record-keeping","Keep processes for access, correction, and deletion requests on record","Rights and exceptions depend on jurisdiction, so document how you respond."],["User rights","Handle customer requests and complaints about their personal data","Mitigation should not block the right to access, correct, or delete data."],["Third-party compliance","Confirm that mitigation providers meet your privacy and ethical standards","Your obligations extend to the vendors that process data for you."]]},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"cards","items":[{"title":"Industry Partnerships","front":"<div data-editor-id=\"85420614-dbea-4391-85c2-77474b37913e\"><p><strong>Industry Partnerships</strong></p></div>","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"2efb1548-58b9-4543-aa61-02599c7cb7dc\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 2rem\">Partner with other businesses, cybersecurity firms, and regulatory bodies to share information about bot threats and mitigation strategies.</span></p></div>"},{"title":"Threat Intelligence Sharing","front":"<div data-editor-id=\"68955e6b-4e61-47c2-a7ee-5b5e7ba74fe2\"><p><strong>Threat Intelligence Sharing</strong></p></div>","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"728d3c40-20b5-432e-8752-684dcfbd874d\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 2rem\">Participate in threat intelligence sharing initiatives to stay informed about the latest bot attack trends and techniques.</span></p></div>"},{"title":"Best Practices Exchange","front":"<div data-editor-id=\"5b74c533-df97-4eec-a86f-7b3038e5c63e\"><p><strong>Best Practices Exchange</strong></p></div>","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"38b41a86-5fdd-46c0-ad43-d1a42da44654\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 2rem\">Engage in industry forums and working groups to exchange best practices and learn from the experiences of other businesses.</span></p></div>"}],"source":["cm2nfg27g06zr3579qakvnsmm","cm2nfdf0k06xd357946xo87lz"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Collaboration and Information Sharing","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-106","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":22},{"lesson":6,"screen":23},{"lesson":6,"screen":24}],"display":"accordion","leadHtml":"<div data-editor-id=\"23fc281e-c102-43b6-a8f7-b423b1d174c1\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Collaborating with industry partners and sharing threat intelligence can enhance your bot management strategies while ensuring compliance with legal and ethical standards. It helps businesses stay ahead of evolving bot threats and adopt best practices in bot management.</span></p></div>","leadImage":null,"leadCaption":""},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>Before deploying device fingerprinting across the site, which question should legal and privacy teams settle first?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>The lawful basis for processing and any consent rules for device access.</p>","correct":true},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Whether the vendor's detection rate is higher than its main competitors.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"2","title":"<p>Whether the fingerprinting script slows page loads on older mobile phones.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"3","title":"<p>Whether other merchants in the industry already use the same vendor tool.</p>","correct":false}],"feedback":"<p>Fingerprinting processes personal data and may access information on the device, so the lawful basis and any consent rules must be settled first. Detection rates, performance, and peer adoption matter, but they are not legal questions.</p>","multi":false,"source":["ioI7TaBtw4DfYUVTNyU7e3s4YTXUHcsS","Srv1Ll3RMucyjodz1E5JrqYAlTCGI60h"],"lessonIndex":5,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-107","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":25}],"fixedOrder":true},{"id":"BOT-SCENARIO-6","lessonIndex":5,"kind":"scenario","character":"assets/scenario-characters/lesson-6.png","title":"Scenario: A Proportionate Response","name":"Sofia","role":"merchant risk manager","stages":[{"context":"You are Sofia, a merchant risk manager. Bot abuse targets account registration and checkout. Budget is limited, and support is already handling complaints about verification friction.","prompt":"Where do you start the mitigation plan?","options":[{"text":"Rank affected journeys by fraud loss and customer impact, assign owners, and choose proportionate controls for each risk.","good":true,"outcome":"The team prioritizes the most damaging abuse and agrees who will act. The plan connects each control to a business risk and a customer outcome."},{"text":"Apply the strictest available challenge to every customer journey and measure the plan only by total requests blocked.","good":false,"outcome":"Genuine users face extra friction even where risk is low. A uniform response can reduce sales without showing whether the highest-impact abuse has been addressed."},{"text":"Buy the lowest-cost tool first, postpone asset mapping, and let each department decide independently which incidents deserve attention.","good":false,"outcome":"Gaps and duplicated effort emerge because the team lacks shared priorities. Tool cost alone does not establish which journeys need protection or who owns the response."}]},{"context":"A proposed detection feature requests detailed device data and long retention. The team wants to activate it before reviewing the collection.","goodLead":"The agreed risk plan gives you a basis for judging whether the data is necessary.","badLead":"Before adding another control, the team returns to the purpose and impact of the plan.","prompt":"What is your next decision?","options":[{"text":"Enable all proposed collection indefinitely because a security purpose automatically resolves every privacy and user-rights concern.","good":false,"outcome":"The team collects data without establishing necessity or retention limits. A security purpose does not remove the need to review privacy, rights, and applicable requirements."},{"text":"Copy the vendor default settings and ask customer support to resolve privacy concerns after customers submit complaints.","good":false,"outcome":"Unreviewed defaults may collect or retain more than the merchant needs. Waiting for complaints leaves the team without a documented and proportionate data decision."},{"text":"Review necessity, access, retention, and user rights with legal and privacy teams before enabling the proposed collection.","good":true,"outcome":"The team limits collection and retention to justified needs and documents the decision. Legal and privacy review helps align the control with applicable requirements and user rights."}]}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<div data-editor-id=\"e55b5f05-3925-4329-b8f9-c9b822230391\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Strategic bot management involves assessing risks to digital assets, evaluating potential threats, and developing a mitigation plan that balances security, business performance, and customer experience.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Use a risk-based decision process: identify the protected customer journey and business impact, monitor relevant signals, choose a proportionate action, and measure both fraud reduction and legitimate-customer friction. Responses may range from observation and slowing requests to targeted challenges, step-up verification, or blocking clearly abusive activity. Assign owners across security, fraud, payments, operations, and customer experience.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-108.svg","alt":"Takeaways: assess risk by journey, choose proportionate actions, respect legal and privacy rules, assign owners across teams."},"caption":"","source":["h2RGfprMIxr2U-txwvUa9letV2DVr6vg","V3o8bDXJXa9mQI3UPYbS5r5Ke-MmkM8S","cpX1AOKOoRiOO20KDf_urblNJzylkTVb"],"lessonIndex":5,"title":"Summary","superHeadings":[],"nextHtml":"<div data-editor-id=\"918a9899-27cc-4b06-a8cd-ce07c7529b2d\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 2rem\"><strong>In the next lesson, we will explore partnering with solution providers.</strong></span></p></div>","id":"BOTQA-108","originalScreens":[{"lesson":6,"screen":26}],"layout":"conceptVisual"},{"kind":"intro","title":"Partnering with Solution Providers","objectiveTitle":"Learning Objectives","items":[{"title":"By the end of this lesson, learners will be able to:","date":"","description":"<ul><li><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Determine how to utilize solution providers to make informed decisions about enhancing security and protecting their business assets.</span></p></li></ul>"}],"image":{"src":"assets/LearnUpon_Thumbnail_Templa-7.jpg","alt":""},"caption":"<div data-editor-id=\"eba8aa2a-364a-46fb-9940-83a34297f74f\"><p><strong>Lesson 7 of 8</strong></p></div>","source":["MsrOqL5WUGamTChsFEgnoWUEjk5iNmHt","Nu5j9heuJOgpLyILsSgIDyIL9RZU0jY1","TL_L-4nYTzePA_MfSL5oj90Hdq1zlyLT"],"lessonIndex":6,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-109","originalScreens":[{"lesson":7,"screen":1}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Choosing the right bot management solution is critical for protecting your business from the ever-evolving threat of bot attacks. We will explore the key features to look for in a bot management solution, criteria for evaluating vendors, and best practices for vendor partnerships. By understanding these aspects, businesses can make informed decisions that enhance their security posture and protect their business assets.</span></p><div class=\"xKey\"><p class=\"xKeyHead\">In this lesson</p><ul><li>The key features to look for in a bot management solution.</li><li>How to evaluate vendors on your own traffic and risks.</li><li>Best practices for a strong, long-term vendor partnership.</li></ul></div>","source":["UfqS4x2nKEXZ7CqR58moAxEzh8GZypsj","JVI2IwT-BBkusAEO1P1EDnRi6JHxZEdm"],"lessonIndex":6,"title":"Partnering with Solution Providers","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-110","originalScreens":[{"lesson":7,"screen":2}],"layout":"conceptVisual","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-110.svg","alt":"Four steps: define needs, compare key features, evaluate vendors with a proof of concept, build the partnership."}},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"process","items":[{"title":"Overview","description":"<p>Evaluate a combination of detection, enforcement, reporting, and operational support. Evidence from your environment is more useful than an unqualified claim of perfect classification.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text.png","alt":""}},{"title":"Real-Time Detection and Response","description":"<p>Test latency and response at the edge and application. Confirm which endpoints and actions are protected, including direct APIs. Real-time decisions still need appropriate evidence and proportionate controls.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Client and Server-Side Detection","description":"<p>Client signals may be missing or manipulated. Servers and edge services can act in real time and provide complementary evidence. Neither detection location guarantees authentic signals or legitimate transactions.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Machine Learning and Behavioral Analysis","description":"<p>These methods can recognize patterns, but depend on data quality and evaluation. Ask about drift, false positives, missed attacks, representative testing, and controlled updates.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Device and Behavioral Fingerprinting","description":"<p>Treat fingerprints as probabilistic evidence rather than guaranteed identities. Evaluate shared environments, changing signals, evasion, privacy, and how results connect to business outcomes.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Challenge-Response Mechanisms","description":"<p>Distinguish visible tasks, managed checks, scores, and attestation. Validate results appropriately on the server. Test accessibility and fallback, and retain authentication and fraud controls after a challenge passes.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Reporting and Analysis","description":"<p>Require views by journey, action, and customer segment. Measure confirmed abuse, customer completion, challenge abandonment, support cases, and latency. Block counts alone do not establish effectiveness.</p>","image":null},{"title":"API and Integration Coverage","description":"<p>Test browser, mobile-app, and API paths, including missing telemetry and alternate routes. Agree integration ownership and failure behavior. An API feature list does not prove that your sensitive actions are protected.</p>","image":null},{"title":"Controlled Customization","description":"<p>Keep merchant ownership of access policy and risk tolerance. Agree change approval, testing, monitoring, audit logs, and rollback. Vendor-managed updates and controlled merchant tuning can work together.</p>","image":null}],"source":["iu5DMJIezJrfhy9iSjLF_fIwcA7UDaY3","Msl5Rbi1zRmk06uTV7avZw_SzPzsw30w","cm0y33fow013g3574hoyngylc"],"lessonIndex":6,"title":"Key Features for Bot Management Solutions","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-111","originalScreens":[{"lesson":7,"screen":3},{"lesson":7,"screen":4},{"lesson":7,"screen":5}],"display":"topics"},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Evaluating potential vendors and establishing strong partnerships are critical steps in implementing an effective bot management solution. Understanding the criteria for evaluation and best practices for vendor collaboration can help you choose the right partner.</span></p>","source":["qbhf1Jp2uuLF1xFGrf6eaZguL5L-9plN","cm0y4mywc01ol35743g43tfxa"],"lessonIndex":6,"title":"Vendor Evaluation","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-112","originalScreens":[{"lesson":7,"screen":6}],"layout":"lessonVisual","image":{"src":"assets/lesson-7-screen-4.png","alt":"A team comparing security providers on protection, integration, and support."}},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"timeline","items":[{"title":"","date":"Efficacy","description":"<p>Run a representative pilot against a documented baseline. Cover browser, mobile-app, and API traffic; known attack examples; approved bots; missing telemetry; and distributed low-rate abuse. Compare confirmed abuse, genuine-customer completion, and operational impact.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Untitled (200 x 200 px) --32.png","alt":""}},{"title":"","date":"Experience and Reputation","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"ac478d23-a38d-4eea-ad21-42bdfc33e425\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">When evaluating a vendor’s experience in bot management, assess their track record by reviewing their years of industry expertise, the scale of clients they serve, and the success stories in managing complex bot threats. Check customer testimonials, case studies, and independent reviews to understand how well their solution has worked in real-world scenarios.</span></p><p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">A vendor with a strong reputation will also likely be involved in industry collaborations and thought leadership, which can provide additional confidence in their capabilities.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (2).png","alt":""}},{"title":"","date":"Technological Capabilities","description":"<p>Evaluate data quality, detection coverage, latency, and resilience as well as algorithms. Ask how models and rules are tested, changed, and rolled back. Inspect the evidence behind claims of accuracy.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (3).png","alt":""}},{"title":"","date":"Scalability and Flexibility","description":"<p>Test realistic traffic, peak events, and provider outages. Agree failure behavior for each journey, integration ownership, and operational limits. Neither fail-open nor fail-closed is appropriate for every action.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (4).png","alt":""}},{"title":"","date":"Support and Service Levels","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"eb55c458-9df8-40a1-ba58-49fe00e92777\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Evaluate the quality of the vendor’s customer support, including the availability of dedicated support teams, response times, and technical expertise. Look for vendors that provide 24/7 support, with tiered service level agreements (SLAs) that outline specific response times for critical issues.</span></p><p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Comprehensive support includes not only troubleshooting but also assistance with implementation, training, and ongoing performance monitoring. Vendors with a proven commitment to support will provide value beyond just the product itself.</span></p></div>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (5).png","alt":""}},{"title":"","date":"Compliance and Security Standards","description":"<p>Review applicable privacy and payment-security responsibilities, data locations, retention, subprocessors, and contractual safeguards. A SOC 2 report is an attestation report, not a certification; inspect scope, period, exceptions, and customer responsibilities. Check the scope of any ISO certification rather than assuming it proves bot-detection efficacy.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (6).png","alt":""}},{"title":"","date":"Total Cost of Ownership","description":"<p>Include implementation, tuning, support, false-positive costs, and operational effort. Agree data export, deletion, transition support, and termination requirements before commitment.</p>","image":{"src":"assets/Your paragraph text (7).png","alt":""}}],"source":["cm0y4p63b01qf3574ol5jakdi"],"lessonIndex":6,"title":"Vendor Evaluation","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-113","originalScreens":[{"lesson":7,"screen":7},{"lesson":7,"screen":8}],"display":"topics"},{"kind":"quiz","title":"Knowledge Check","question":"<p>Two vendors both claim to stop 99 percent of bots. Which step best shows which one will work for your business?</p>","answers":[{"id":"0","title":"<p>Choosing the vendor with the most customers, since it sees the most attacks.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"1","title":"<p>Comparing their published detection rates and picking the higher figure.</p>","correct":false},{"id":"2","title":"<p>A proof of concept on your own browser, app, and API traffic, with error rates.</p>","correct":true},{"id":"3","title":"<p>Selecting the cheaper vendor, since both claim the same level of protection.</p>","correct":false}],"feedback":"<p>Headline claims are not comparable across vendors. A representative proof of concept on your own traffic shows missed abuse, false positives, and customer friction in your environment, which customer counts, published rates, or price cannot.</p>","multi":false,"source":["Bw6Yfz3a0z5GV9syVOJkGi1KsRkiBGz4","m5Vs6E1ofU2usnuTgmBEu_ecx3bLzI0L"],"lessonIndex":6,"superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-114","originalScreens":[{"lesson":7,"screen":9}],"fixedOrder":true},{"kind":"activity","source":["pBFk2CcbxvClPJ3PyKUKjzPx5A6TZfOd","6S8f0U7fOP6FvsrI3mu6e18EKuCm4iP7","q3FhmQoyHwdOUZwInG9lHN0uOmlkM4SR"],"lessonIndex":6,"title":"Best Practices in Vendor Partnership","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-115","originalScreens":[{"lesson":7,"screen":10},{"lesson":7,"screen":11}],"leadHtml":"<p>A strong partnership with your bot management vendor keeps the solution adaptable as needs change.</p>","display":"match","pairs":[["Clear communication","Review confirmed abuse, completion, and latency by journey against the baseline","Shared metrics keep both sides focused on real outcomes."],["Regular reviews and feedback","Agree responsibilities, approvals, emergency contacts, and rollback steps","Clear ownership speeds up decisions when something goes wrong."],["Collaborative planning","Plan updates, new features, and security patches together","Joint planning ensures smooth integration and proactive responses."],["Training and support","Train your team from basic use to advanced features","A well-trained team responds to threats faster."]]},{"id":"BOT-SCENARIO-7","lessonIndex":6,"kind":"scenario","character":"assets/scenario-characters/lesson-7.png","title":"Scenario: Beyond the Vendor Promise","name":"Ethan","role":"fraud technology lead","stages":[{"context":"You are Ethan, a fraud technology lead comparing bot vendors. One advertises the highest detection rate. Another offers a pilot across your browser, mobile-app, and API journeys.","prompt":"What evidence do you request?","options":[{"text":"Select the highest advertised percentage and postpone testing until every customer journey has moved to the new service.","good":false,"outcome":"The headline claim does not reveal performance on your traffic or customer friction. Problems may appear only after the business depends on the service."},{"text":"Run a representative pilot measuring missed abuse, false positives, customer friction, and operational fit across those journeys.","good":true,"outcome":"The pilot shows how each solution performs in your environment. Verified outcomes and operational fit give the team a stronger basis than an untested marketing percentage."},{"text":"Choose the fastest demonstration response and compare vendors only on how many requests their sample dashboards can block.","good":false,"outcome":"A demonstration may not reflect your traffic or integrations. A large block count can conceal missed attacks or genuine customers incorrectly denied access."}]},{"context":"The pilot is promising, but the proposed agreement leaves incident escalation, reporting, retention, and exit arrangements unclear.","goodLead":"The pilot evidence supports continued evaluation rather than an immediate full rollout.","badLead":"The team now requires representative evidence before proceeding with the promising pilot.","prompt":"How do you prepare the partnership?","options":[{"text":"Agree on support ownership, incident escalation, reporting, data terms, and exit requirements before completing the rollout plan.","good":true,"outcome":"Both teams know how to respond, measure performance, and handle data and service changes. Clear responsibilities make the partnership more resilient after implementation."},{"text":"Accept the standard terms immediately and rely on the sales contact to coordinate every future incident and data request.","good":false,"outcome":"Operational responsibilities remain uncertain when an incident occurs. A sales relationship alone does not define support, reporting, data handling, or a workable exit."},{"text":"Remove reporting requirements to speed deployment and review incident responsibilities only after the first major service disruption occurs.","good":false,"outcome":"The merchant lacks agreed evidence and escalation when problems arise. Speeding deployment by omitting responsibilities creates avoidable uncertainty during an incident."}]}]},{"kind":"image","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Choosing the right bot management solution is crucial for protecting your business from evolving bot threats. Strong providers support layered detection, adaptable enforcement, reliable reporting, and collaborative operational support.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">A representative proof of concept should cover browser, mobile-app, and API traffic. Evaluate documented detection methodology, false positives and negatives, customer friction, reporting, incident support, adaptability, integrations, privacy, data retention, availability, and exit requirements. Prefer the provider that demonstrates better risk-adjusted protection in your environment rather than the one promising the largest unverified percentage.</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-116.svg","alt":"Takeaways: look for layered detection, test with a proof of concept, measure errors and friction, check support, privacy, and exit terms."},"caption":"","source":["EL5mjifRLWhpt3G1U4EqeQSauB9Wze3-","2TAqXUQDj9CsACzRlu0e9p5U5Ors-Qgt","rIGuu6hJ-9EiCZJ1_6WlSZvha1WtZ_L0"],"lessonIndex":6,"title":"Summary","superHeadings":[],"nextHtml":"<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 20px;\">In the final lesson, <strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong><strong>we will have a brief review of the course&rsquo;s key learnings.</strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></strong></span></strong></p>","id":"BOTQA-116","originalScreens":[{"lesson":7,"screen":12}],"layout":"conceptVisual"},{"kind":"image","id":"BOTQA-117-intro","lessonIndex":7,"title":"Course Review","html":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Congratulations! You have completed all modules of the course. Please review the course key learnings before exiting to take the final exam.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As you review, consider the threat type, the affected customer journey, the evidence, the proposed control, and the expected business outcome. Avoid treating a single signal as proof of malicious automation.</span></p>","image":{"src":"assets/concepts/botqa-117-intro.svg","alt":"Review checklist: threat type, affected customer journey, evidence, proposed control, expected business outcome."},"source":["cl2jkd45c000g3a6quv8oebrz","clbo7bxq10007356qhui6x03v","cl2jkbw5a00033a6q8ued2zpt","clbo7ah660003356q0y2duhli"],"originalScreens":[{"lesson":8,"screen":1},{"lesson":8,"screen":2},{"lesson":8,"screen":3}],"layout":"conceptVisual"},{"kind":"interaction","variant":"process","items":[{"title":"Introduction to eCommerce Bot Attacks","date":"","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"f206e03e-086f-4629-a99b-81d67cc3cdfc\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Bot attacks are a longstanding yet unresolved issue in eCommerce, growing more sophisticated as attackers automate more of the customer journey. These threats can cause financial damage, disrupt operations, and erode customer trust.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Modern malicious automation ranges from simple scripted requests to sophisticated activity that imitates legitimate shopping. AI-assisted tools can help attackers develop or adjust scripts and react to defenses, but not every advanced bot uses AI. Evaluate the specific journey at risk, such as login, account creation, product discovery, inventory reservation, checkout, loyalty redemption, or payment, and compare suspicious activity with business context before labeling it an attack.</span></p></div>","image":null},{"title":"Types of Bot Attacks","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bots are automated programs that can serve either beneficial or harmful purposes online. Good bots help with tasks such as web crawling and monitoring, while bad bots support abuse such as scraping, spamming, credential stuffing, account takeover, loyalty abuse, scalping, DDoS, and payment-card testing.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Modern automated abuse can target browser, mobile-app, and API flows, including login, registration, product, inventory, checkout, loyalty, and payment endpoints. Different attacks may share automation techniques but create different business risks, so classification should reflect the affected customer journey and business outcome, not just the presence of automation.</span></p>","image":null},{"title":"Signs and Symptoms of a Bot Attack","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bot attacks are becoming increasingly sophisticated, making early detection crucial to protect digital infrastructure. Common indicators include sudden shifts in traffic, unusual behavior patterns, repeated failures, abnormal account or payment activity, and changes in customer experience metrics.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A spike in pageviews alone does not establish malicious automation. Review request velocity, unusual paths, repeated failures, device and network patterns, account creation rates, checkout completion, payment authorization outcomes, and business context such as marketing campaigns, product launches, and seasonal activity. Sophisticated automation can also occur without a dramatic traffic spike.</span></p>","image":null},{"title":"Traditional Bot Management Techniques","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Traditional bot management techniques, such as CAPTCHAs, IP restrictions, deny or allow lists, device signals, and rate limiting, still play an important role in eCommerce defenses.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">These controls remain useful, but none is a complete safeguard on its own. Distributed actors may rotate IP addresses, CAPTCHA solvers can defeat challenges, and device fingerprints are probabilistic rather than guaranteed identities. Rate limits should be tailored to the endpoint, account, device, network, and business context, with ongoing monitoring for legitimate-customer friction.</span></p>","image":null},{"title":"Current Bot Management Techniques","date":"","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"e7cd48c7-018f-4b1c-9e1a-8ae7d0fee878\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">As bot attacks become more advanced, organizations increasingly rely on layered detection and response rather than any single signal or control. Current solutions combine behavior analysis, machine learning, request evaluation, and enforcement across the customer journey.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Client-side telemetry can describe browser or app behavior, but data generated on a client may be altered or imitated. Server-side and edge defenses can also respond in real time when the architecture supports it. Effective programs layer request, behavior, identity, transaction, and verified client signals, and they evaluate models for signal quality, drift, false positives, false negatives, and adversarial manipulation.</span></p></div>","image":null},{"title":"Strategic Considerations for Bot Management","date":"","description":"<div data-editor-id=\"e55b5f05-3925-4329-b8f9-c9b822230391\"><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Strategic bot management involves assessing risks to digital assets, evaluating potential threats, and developing a mitigation plan that balances security, business performance, and customer experience.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Use a risk-based decision process: identify the protected customer journey and business impact, monitor relevant signals, choose a proportionate action, and measure both fraud reduction and legitimate-customer friction. Responses may range from observation and slowing requests to targeted challenges, step-up verification, or blocking clearly abusive activity. Assign owners across security, fraud, payments, operations, and customer experience.</span></p></div>","image":null},{"title":"Partnering with Solution Providers","date":"","description":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">Choosing the right bot management solution is crucial for protecting your business from evolving bot threats. Strong providers support layered detection, adaptable enforcement, reliable reporting, and collaborative operational support.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 1.8rem\">A representative proof of concept should cover browser, mobile-app, and API traffic. Evaluate documented detection methodology, false positives and negatives, customer friction, reporting, incident support, adaptability, integrations, privacy, data retention, availability, and exit requirements. Prefer the provider that demonstrates better risk-adjusted protection in your environment rather than the one promising the largest unverified percentage.</span></p>","image":null}],"source":["cl2jkd45c000g3a6quv8oebrz","clbo7bxq10007356qhui6x03v","cl2jkbw5a00033a6q8ued2zpt","clbo7ah660003356q0y2duhli"],"lessonIndex":7,"title":"Review","superHeadings":[],"id":"BOTQA-117","originalScreens":[{"lesson":8,"screen":1},{"lesson":8,"screen":2},{"lesson":8,"screen":3}],"display":"review"}],"revision":"botactsaud20261106","counts":[{"title":"Introduction to eCommerce Bot Attacks","screens":15},{"title":"Types of Bot Attacks","screens":31},{"title":"Signs and Symptoms of a Bot Attack","screens":15},{"title":"Traditional Bot Management Techniques","screens":19},{"title":"Current Bot Management Solutions","screens":18},{"title":"Strategic Considerations for Bot Management","screens":16},{"title":"Partnering with Solution Providers","screens":9},{"title":"Review","screens":2}]};